1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,980
模型未必把自己看作是在试图预测下一个词，对吧？
The model doesn't necessarily think of itself as trying to predict the next word, right?

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:15,700
在内部，它可能已经形成了各种中间目标和抽象，当你与大语言模型（LLM）对话时，这些会帮助它实现那种元目标。
Internally, it's developed potentially all sorts of intermediate goals and abstractions that help it achieve that kind of meta objective when you're talking to a large language model.

3
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,780
你到底是在和什么对话？
What exactly is it that you're talking to?

4
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:21,100
你是在和一个高级版自动补全对话吗？
Are you talking to something like a glorified autocomplete?

5
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:30,000
还是在和某种互联网搜索引擎，或者某种真正在思考、甚至像人一样思考的东西对话？
Are you talking to something like an internet search engine or are you talking to something that's actually thinking and maybe even thinking like a person?

6
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:35,160
结果相当令人担忧，没有人真正知道这些问题的答案。
It turns out, rather concerningly, that nobody really knows the answer to those questions.

7
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:40,560
在 Anthropic，我们非常想找到这些问题的答案。
And here at Anthropic, we are very interested in finding those answers out.

8
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,040
我们采用的方法是使用可解释性（interpretability）。
The way that we do that is to use interpretability.

9
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:53,260
也就是打开大语言模型、观察内部，并试图弄清它在回答问题时到底发生了什么。
That is the science of opening up a large language model, looking inside and trying to work out what's going on as it's answering your questions.

10
00:00:54,100 --> 00:01:04,700
很高兴请到我们可解释性团队的三位成员，他们会介绍最近关于 Claude 复杂内部运作的研究。
And I'm very glad to be joined by three members of our interpretability team, who are going to tell me a little bit about the recent research that they've been doing on the complex inner workings of Claude.

11
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:05,660
也就是我们的语言模型。
Our language model.

12
00:01:06,090 --> 00:01:08,130
嗯，请你们自我介绍一下吧。
Um, please introduce yourselves, guys.

13
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:09,290
嗨，我是 Jack。
Hi, I'm Jack.

14
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:13,770
我是可解释性团队的研究员，之前是一名神经科学家。
I'm a researcher on the interpretability team and before that I was a neuroscientist.

15
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:16,570
现在我是在对 AI 做神经科学研究。
Now here I am doing neuroscience on the AIs.

16
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:17,530
我是 Emmanuel。
I'm Emmanuel.

17
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:19,330
我也在可解释性团队。
I'm also on the interpretability team.

18
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:23,970
我职业生涯大部分时间在构建机器学习模型，现在尝试理解它们。
I spent most of my career building machine learning models, and now I'm trying to understand them.

19
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:25,170
我是 Josh。
I'm Josh.

20
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:26,970
我也在可解释性团队。
I'm also on the interpretability team.

21
00:01:27,290 --> 00:01:32,080
过去我研究病毒演化，再之前我是一名数学家。
In my past life, I studied viral evolution and in my past past life, I was a mathematician.

22
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:36,920
所以现在我就像是在用生物学研究这些我们用数学造出来的“有机体”。
So now I'm doing this kind of biology on these organisms we've made out of math.

23
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:37,760
等等。
Now, wait a second.

24
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:39,600
你刚刚说在这里做生物学。
You just said you're doing biology here.

25
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,400
很多人会感到惊讶，因为这毕竟是一个软件，对吧？
Now, a lot of people are gonna be surprised by that because, of course, this is a piece of software, right?

26
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,160
但它不是普通软件。
But it's not a normal piece of software.

27
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,040
它不像 Microsoft Word 之类的东西。
It's not like Microsoft Word or something.

28
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:56,700
你能谈谈，你说在一个软件实体上做生物学或神经科学，是什么意思吗？
Can you talk about what you mean when you say that you're doing biology or indeed neuroscience on a software entity?

29
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:02,420
我觉得这更多是它给人的感觉，而不是字面意义上它是什么。
Yeah, I guess it's like what it feels like maybe more than like what it literally is.

30
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:09,700
所以也许可以叫语言模型的生物学，而不是语言模型的物理学，对吧？
And so like it's maybe the biology of language models instead of like the physics of language models, right?

31
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:16,820
或者你得稍微回顾一下模型是如何被制造出来的：并不是有人编程说，如果用户说“嗨”，你就说“嗨”。
Or maybe you gotta go back a little bit to like how the models are made, which is like someone's not programming, like, if the user says hi, you should say hi.

32
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:19,990
比如用户问“什么早餐好？”
You know, if the user says, like, what is a good breakfast?

33
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:20,790
你就说“吐司”。
You should say toast.

34
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:22,430
模型内部并没有这样一张大清单。
There's not, like, some big list of that inside.

35
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:30,150
所以它不像你玩电子游戏时选一个回应，然后会自动出现另一个回应，而且每次都是那个回应。
So it's not like when you play a video game and you, like, choose a response and then there will be another response that comes automatically that always will be that response regardless of, you know.

36
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:33,350
难道不是一个巨大的数据库，涵盖每种情况下该说什么吗？
Just a massive database of like what to say in every situation?

37
00:02:33,630 --> 00:02:39,190
不，它们是训练出来的：大量数据输入进去，然后模型
No, they're trained where there's just, you know, a whole lot of data that goes in and the model.

38
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:47,810
一开始几乎不会说任何有意义的话，然后内部部件在每一个样本上被微调，以更好地预测接下来要说什么。
Starts out being really bad at saying anything and then its inside parts get tweaked, you know, on every single example to get better at saying what comes next.

39
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:49,610
到最后，它极其擅长这件事。
And at the end, it's like extremely good at that.

40
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:58,090
但因为这个过程有点像微调和演化，到结束的时候，它和最初已经几乎没有相似之处。
But because it's like this little tweaking evolutionary process, by the time it's done, it has little resemblance to what it started as.

41
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:00,010
但没有人进去手动设置所有旋钮。
But no one went in and set all the knobs.

42
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:09,230
所以你要研究的是一个随着时间逐渐形成的复杂事物，有点像生物形态随着时间演化。
And so you're trying to study this complicated thing that kind of got made over time, kind of like biological forms evolved over time.

43
00:03:10,590 --> 00:03:16,190
因此它很复杂、很神秘，也很有趣。
And so there's, it's complicated, it's mysterious and it's fun to study.

44
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:21,870
至于它实际在做什么，我一开始提到，这就像被看作自动补全，对吧？
And what it's actually doing, I mean, I mentioned at the start that this is, like, considered an autocomplete, right?

45
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:24,070
它在预测下一个词。
It's, it's, it's predicting the next word.

46
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:26,990
这基本上是模型内部正在发生的事情，对吧？
That's fundamentally what's happening inside the model, right?

47
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:29,310
然而它却能做所有这些不可思议的事。
And yet it's able to do all these incredible things.

48
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:31,230
它会写代码，会写诗。
It's able to code, to write poetry.

49
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:33,590
能写长故事。
It's able to write long stories.

50
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:39,790
还能做加法、基础数学，即使它内部没有计算器。
It's able to do, uh, addition and, you know, basic maths, even though it doesn't have a calculator inside it.

51
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:53,290
那么，我们如何调和这个看似矛盾的事实：它一次只预测一个词，却能做到这些人们一和模型对话就能看到的惊人事情。嗯，我觉得有一点。
So how can we sort of, uh, um, square the circle that it's predicting one word at a time and yet it's able to do all these amazing things, which people can see right in front of them as soon as they talk to the model. Well, I think, I think one.

52
00:03:53,690 --> 00:04:00,170
这里很重要的一点是，当你预测足够多的下一个词时，你会意识到有些词比其他词更难预测。
One thing that's important here is that as you predict the next word for enough words, you realize that some words are harder than others.

53
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:13,430
所以语言模型训练的一部分是预测句子中平淡的词，另一部分是它最终必须学会如何补全等号之后的内容。
And so part, part of, part of, uh, language model training is predicting, you know, boring words in a sentence and part of it is it'll have to eventually learn how to complete what happens after the equal sign in an equation.

54
00:04:13,630 --> 00:04:17,950
要做到这一点，它必须有自己计算的方法。
And to do that, it'll have to have some way of computing that on its own.

55
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:35,000
我们发现，预测下一个词的任务看似简单，但要真正做好，你往往需要思考你正在预测的词之后的词，或者生成你当前正在思考的词的过程。所以这就像一种上下文理解。
And so what we're finding is that the task of predicting the next word is sort of like deceptively simple, and that to do that well, you need to actually often think about the words that come after the word you're predicting or the process that generated the word that you're currently thinking about. So it's like a contextual understanding that.

56
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:46,880
模型必须具备这种能力，它不像自动补全——自动补全里大概没有太多别的机制，只是当你输入“the cat sat on the”，它预测“mat”，因为这个特定短语以前出现过。
These models have to have, and it's not like an autocomplete where it really is presumably there's not much else going on there other than when you write the cat sat on the, it's predicting, mat, because that's been, that particular phrase has been used before.

57
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:49,520
相反，这是模型具备的一种上下文理解。
Instead, it's like a contextual understanding that the model has.

58
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:59,240
我喜欢这样想，继续用生物学类比：从某种意义上说，人类的目标是生存和繁衍。
Yeah, the way I like to think about it, kind of continuing with the biology analogy, is that in one sense, the goal of a human is to survive and reproduce.

59
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:04,280
那是进化塑造我们去实现的目标。
That is the kind of objective that evolution is crafting us to achieve.

60
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:10,600
但这并不是你如何看待自己，也不是你大脑里实际发生的事情。有些人倒是这样。
And yet that's not how you think of yourself and that's not what's going on in your brain. Some people do.

61
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:13,490
这不是你大脑里一直在发生的事。
It's not what's going on in your brain all of the time.

62
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:33,580
你会思考其他事情，思考目标、计划和概念；从某种元层面来看，进化赋予了你形成这些想法的能力，以实现繁殖这个最终目标。
You think about other things and you think about goals and plans and concepts and at kind of a meta level, evolution has endowed you with the ability to form those thoughts in order to achieve this eventual goal of reproduction.

63
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:39,020
但这有点像采取内在视角，也就是从内部来看“作为你”是什么感觉。
But that's kind of taking the inside view, what it's like to be you on the inside.

64
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:41,660
这还不是全部。
That's, that's not all there is to it.

65
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:43,940
还有很多其他事情在发生。
There's, there's all, there's all this other stuff going on.

66
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:50,580
所以，你是说预测下一个词这个最终目标，涉及许多其他正在进行的过程。
And I think, so, so, so you're saying that the ultimate goal of predicting the next word involves lots of other processes that are going on.

67
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:51,340
没错。
Exactly.

68
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:55,820
模型并不一定把自己看作是在试图预测下一个词。
The model doesn't think of itself necessarily as trying to predict the next word.

69
00:05:56,140 --> 00:05:58,540
它是被这个需要塑造出来的。
It's, it's been shaped by the need to do that.

70
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:06,620
但在内部，它可能已经发展出各种中间目标和抽象，来帮助它实现那种元目标。
But internally, it's developed potentially all sorts of intermediate goals and abstractions that help it achieve that kind of meta objective.

71
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:08,020
而且有时这很神秘。
And sometimes it's mysterious.

72
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:19,700
比如，不清楚为什么我的焦虑对我祖先繁衍后代有用，但我不知怎么被赋予了这种内在状态，它必定在某种意义上与进化有关。
Like, it's unclear why my anxiety was useful for my ancestors reproducing, and yet somehow I've been endowed with this internal state that must be related in some sense to evolution.

73
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,120
对，对，对。
Right, right, right.

74
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:29,000
那么可以说，这些模型只是在预测下一个词，但这对模型内部发生的事是极大的低估。
So it's fair to say then that these are just predicting the next word and yet that's, that's to do a massive disservice to what's going on in the models.

75
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:29,280
确实。
Really.

76
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:36,720
从某种意义上说，这既对也不对，或者说，它极大低估了这些模型内部正在发生的事情。
It's, it's both true and also untrue in a, in a, in a, in a sense, or, or, or, um, massively underestimates what's happening inside these models.

77
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:41,560
也许我会这样说：这是对的，但它不是理解它们如何运作最有用的视角，对吧？
Maybe the way I would say this is it's true, but it's not the most useful lens to try to understand how they work, right?

78
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,280
那么，试着理解它们如何运作。
So, well, try and understand how they work.

79
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:46,600
你们团队会做些什么来尝试理解它们如何运作？
What do you guys do in your team to try and understand how they work?

80
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:55,200
我认为，粗略地说，我们想做的是告诉你模型的思考过程。
I think to a first approximation, what we're trying to do is tell you the model's thought process.

81
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:01,720
你给模型一串词，它就必须吐出点东西来。
So you give the model a sequence of words and it's got to spit something, spit something out.

82
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,680
它必须说出一个词，说出一串词来回应你的问题。
It's got to say a word, it's got to say a string of words in response to your question.

83
00:07:06,110 --> 00:07:06,910
对。
Yeah.

84
00:07:06,910 --> 00:07:08,950
我们想知道它是怎么从 A 到 B 的。
And we want to know how it got from A to B.

85
00:07:09,590 --> 00:07:31,460
我们认为，在从 A 到 B 的过程中，它会经历一系列步骤，可以说在这些步骤中思考概念，比如像具体物体和词语这样的底层概念，以及像它的目标这样的更高层概念，或者……
And we think that on the way from A to B, it uses kind of a series of steps in which it's thinking about, you know, so to speak concepts, concepts like low level concepts like individual kind of objects and words and higher level concepts like its goals or, you know.

86
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:38,100
情绪状态，或者对用户在想什么的建模，或者情感倾向。
Emotional states or models of like what the user is thinking or sentiments.

87
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:48,420
所以它使用这样一系列概念，这些概念沿着模型的计算步骤推进，帮助它决定最终答案。
So it's using this kind of series of concepts that are progressing through the kind of computational steps of the model that help it decide on its final answer.

88
00:07:48,500 --> 00:08:02,800
我们想做的基本上是给你一张流程图，告诉你哪些概念按什么顺序被使用，哪些概念导致了后续步骤，这些步骤是如何彼此衔接的。
And what we're trying to do is kind of give you a, a flowchart, basically, that tells you, you know, which concepts were being used in which order and which ones kind of led, you know, how did the steps flow into one another?

89
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:05,000
但我们要怎么知道这一点呢？
How do, how do we know that, though?

90
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,080
我们一开始怎么知道存在这些概念呢？
How do we know that there are these concepts in the first place?

91
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:17,720
对，我们做的一件事是，我们其实能看到模型内部，我们有访问权限，所以你差不多可以看到模型的哪些部分在做哪些事情。
Yeah, so, so one thing we do is, is that sort of we actually can see inside, inside the model, we have access to it so you can sort of like see which parts of the model do which things.

92
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:23,290
我们不知道的是，这些部分如何组合在一起，以及它们是否映射到某个概念，对吧？
What we don't know is, like, how these parts are grouped together and if they map to, like, a certain concept, right?

93
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:27,010
这就像你打开某人的头，在里面你能看到类似 fMRI 的图像。
So it's as if you open someone's head and there you could see like one of those fMRI.

94
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:31,690
在那些脑成像里，你能看到大脑在亮起来，在做各种——显然有事情在发生。
Brain images that you could see the brain was like lighting up and doing all sorts of, something's happening clearly.

95
00:08:31,810 --> 00:08:32,050
对。
Right.

96
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:32,330
但你。
But you.

97
00:08:32,410 --> 00:08:33,370
然后它们就像是在做各种事情。
And then they're like doing stuff.

98
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:34,570
有事情在发生。
There's something happening.

99
00:08:35,010 --> 00:08:35,610
把大脑取出来。
Take the brain out.

100
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:36,330
它们就不活动了。
They stop doing stuff.

101
00:08:36,450 --> 00:08:37,690
对，大脑一定很重要。
Yeah, brain must be important.

102
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:38,010
对。
Yeah.

103
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:44,450
但是你没有一把钥匙，可以用来理解那个大脑内部发生了什么。
And, but, but you, but you don't have a sort of key to understand what is happening inside that brain.

104
00:08:44,530 --> 00:08:49,260
对，不过再把这个类比硬套一下，你差不多可以想象。
Yeah, but torturing that analogy a little bit, you, you can sort of imagine, imagine like that.

105
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:56,780
你可以观察他们的大脑，然后发现，比如那个部分总是在他们端起咖啡杯时亮起，另一个部分总是在他们喝茶时亮起。
You can, you know, observe their brain and then see that, like that part always lights up when they're picking up a cup of coffee and this other part always lights up when they're drinking tea.

106
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:05,440
这也是我们试图理解这些组件各自在做什么的方式之一：就是观察它们什么时候活跃，什么时候不活跃。
And that's part, that's one of the ways in which we can try to understand what each of these components are doing is to just notice when they're active, when they're inactive.

107
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:07,400
而且不是只有一个部分，对吧？
And it's not that there's just one part, right?

108
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:09,760
有很多不同部分会亮起来，对吧？
There's, there's many different parts that light up, right?

109
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:14,000
比如，当模型在想着喝咖啡之类的事情时，对吧？
When the model is thinking about drinking coffee, for instance, or, or something, right?

110
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:22,120
工作的一部分就是把这些部分拼接成一个整体，然后说，啊，这就是模型里与喝咖啡有关的所有小片段，对吧？
And part of the work is to sort of like stitch all of those together into one ensemble that we say is, ah, this is the sort of like all of the bits of the model that are about drinking coffee, right?

111
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:25,360
那么，这在科学上是件简单直接的事吗？
And, and is that like a straightforward scientifically thing to do?

112
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:31,720
比如，当面对这些庞大模型时，它们一定有无穷无尽的概念，对吧？
Like, uh, how, how, uh, you know, when it comes to, when it comes to one of these massive models, they must have endless concepts, right?

113
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:33,760
而且它们一定能想到无穷无尽的事物。
And, uh, they must, they must be able to think of endless things.

114
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:37,760
你可以输入任何短语，它都会生成无限的内容。
You can put in any phrase you want and it'll come up with infinite things.

115
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:40,840
你们甚至是怎么开始找到所有这些概念的？
How, how do you even begin to find all those concepts?

116
00:09:41,940 --> 00:09:59,460
我想这是这个研究领域多年来一直面临的核心挑战之一：我们作为人类可以进去说，哦，我打赌模型对火车有某种表征，或者我打赌它对爱有某种表征。
I think that's been kind of one of the central challenges for this research field for many years now, is we can kind of go in as humans and say, oh, I bet the model has some representation of trains or I bet it has some representation of love.

117
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:01,100
但我们只是在猜。
But we're just kind of guessing.

118
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:12,220
所以我们真正想要的是一种方法，来揭示模型自身使用了哪些抽象，而不是把我们自己的概念框架强加在它身上。
So what we really want is a way to, you know, reveal what abstractions the model uses itself rather than sort of imposing our own conceptual framework on it.

119
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:26,660
这差不多就是我们研究方法旨在做的事情：尽量以无假设的方式，把模型头脑中的所有概念浮现出来。
And that's kind of what our research methods are designed to do is in a sort of hypothesis free, as much as possible way, bring to the surface what all these concepts are that the model has in its head.

120
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:29,180
而且我们经常发现，它们会让我们感到意外。
And often we find that they're kind of surprising to us.

121
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:30,410
它们可能就是这类东西。
They might be it.

122
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:34,530
它可能会用一些从人类角度看有点奇怪的抽象。
It might sort of use abstractions that are a bit weird from a human perspective.

123
00:10:34,650 --> 00:10:35,290
举个例子？
What's an example?

124
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:37,090
你们有没有一个最喜欢的，或者……
Do you—do you have a favorite or...?

125
00:10:37,610 --> 00:10:40,130
我们的论文里有很多，我们挑了几个好玩的。
There's lots in our papers, we highlight a few fun ones.

126
00:10:40,130 --> 00:10:51,130
我觉得有一个特别有趣的是那种迎合式称赞的特征：模型里会有那么一部分，会对“好例子，绝妙的例子，绝对绝佳的例子，哦谢谢”这类话有反应。
I think one, one that was particularly funny is the sort of like sycophantic praise one, where like there, there is a part of the model, like, "Great example, what a brilliant example. What an absolutely fantastic example. Oh, thank you."

127
00:10:52,250 --> 00:10:53,130
就是有这么一个部分。
There's a part of that.

128
00:10:53,250 --> 00:10:56,850
就是那个部分会在这些情境下激活，对吧？
There's a part of that that activates in exactly these contexts, right?

129
00:10:57,170 --> 00:11:03,610
你能清楚地看到，天哪，当有人真的在猛灌赞美话时，模型的这部分就会亮起来。
And you can clearly see, oh, man, this part of the model fires up when, when somebody's really piling on the compliments.

130
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:07,850
这有点令人惊讶，它竟然作为一个挺具体的概念存在。
That's, that's kind of surprising that, that exists as a specific sort of concept.

131
00:11:08,610 --> 00:11:10,570
Josh，你最喜欢的概念是什么？
Josh, what's your favorite concept?

132
00:11:11,370 --> 00:11:14,250
哦，这就好比你让我从我的 3000 万个孩子里选一个。
Oh, it's like asking me to choose one of my 30 million children.

133
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:20,800
我觉得，嗯，喜欢的概念可能有两种。
I mean, I, I think, you know, there, there's like two kinds of favorites.

134
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:28,120
一种是，哇，它竟然能对某个很小的东西有某种特别的概念，你懂吧？
There's like, oh, it's so cool that it's got some special notion of like this one, you know, little thing, right?

135
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:32,280
比如我们在金门大桥上做过研究，它是旧金山一个著名地标。
I mean we did this thing on the Golden Gate Bridge, which is, like, a famous San Francisco landmark.

136
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:33,000
Golden Gate Claude。
Golden Gate Claude.

137
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:33,880
这真的很好玩。
It's like a lot of fun.

138
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:47,700
它对金门大桥的理解不只是“Golden Gate”后面自动补全“Bridge”这几个字，而是像“我在从旧金山开车去 Marin”的时候，它在想的是同一个东西。
It, like, has an idea of the Golden Gate Bridge that, like, isn't just like the words "Golden Gate" autocomplete "Bridge", but is like, "I'm driving from San Francisco to Marin" and then it's thinking of the same thing.

139
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:56,300
也就是说，你会看到内部有同样的东西亮起来，或者它像是对这座桥有一个图像；于是你会觉得，好吧，它对这座桥是什么有了一个挺稳固的概念。
Meaning that like you see sort of like the same stuff light up inside, or it's like a picture of the bridge, and so you're like, okay, it's got some robust notion of like what the bridge is.

140
00:11:56,540 --> 00:12:05,980
但我认为，对于更奇怪的现象，比如一个问题是：模型是如何跟踪故事里都有谁的？
But I think when it comes to, um, stuff that seems sort of like weirder, you know, one question is how, how do models, like, keep track of who's in the story?

141
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:09,260
就像，字面意义上，好吧，故事里有一堆人，他们在做各种事。
Like just like literally like, like, okay, you've got all these people and they're doing stuff.

142
00:12:09,260 --> 00:12:14,180
模型是怎么把这些信息连接起来的？一些其他实验室的有趣论文显示，也许它们只是给人编个号。
How do you wire that together? And some cool papers by other labs showing, well, maybe like they just sort of number them.

143
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:18,060
比如第一个人出现后，任何和他相关的事，模型就只当作“第一个人做了这件事”。
Okay, the first person comes in and anything associated with them, they just like, oh, the first guy did that.

144
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:19,180
它脑子里就像有个“第 2 号”
And like it's got like a No. 2

145
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:20,340
用来标记其中很多信息。
in its head for a bunch of those.

146
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:21,880
你会觉得，哦，这挺有意思。
And it's like, oh, that's, that's interesting.

147
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:22,520
我之前不知道。
I didn't know.

148
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:24,160
我不知道它会做这种事。
I didn't know it would do something like that.

149
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:29,600
还有一个特征是关于代码里的 bug。
There was, um, uh, a feature for like bugs in code.

150
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:31,320
软件会有错误。
So, you know, software has mistakes.

151
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,960
不是我的，不过显然也不是你的。
Not mine, but like, obviously not yours.

152
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:34,760
肯定不是我的。
Not mine, certainly.

153
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:39,560
有一个部分会在它阅读过程中发现错误时亮起来。
And there was, like, one part that would light up, like, whenever it found, like, a mistake, sort of as it was reading.

154
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:47,340
然后我猜测，它有点像是在跟踪这些信息：问题在这里，之后可能会用到；这只是再给大家一些例子。
And then I guess, like, keeping kind of track of that, like, oh, here's where the problems are, you know, and later I might need those, just to give a flavor for, for a few more of these.

155
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:59,300
还有一个我特别喜欢的，一开始听上去不那么激动人心，但我觉得挺深刻的，就是模型内部的这个“6 + 9”特征。
I think, um, one, one that I really liked, which doesn't sound so exciting at first, but I think is kind of deep, is this 6 + 9 feature inside the model.

156
00:12:59,660 --> 00:13:10,260
结果是，只要你让模型把一个以 6 结尾的数和一个以 9 结尾的数相加。
Um, so it turns out that, like, anytime you get the model to be adding a number that ends in the digit 6, and another number that ends in the digit 9.

157
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:11,260
在它脑中做这个加法时。
Right in its head.

158
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:22,120
模型大脑中的某个部分就会亮起来；但令人惊讶的是它能出现在非常多样的上下文中。
There is, you know, there's a kind of part of the model's brain that lights up, and, but what's amazing about it is the kind of diversity of context in which this can happen.

159
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:27,920
你直接问 6 + 9 等于多少，它回答 15 时，它当然会亮起来。
So like, of course it's going to light up when you present, when you say like 6 + 9 equals and then it says 15.

160
00:13:28,760 --> 00:13:46,470
但当你在写论文时给出一个引用，引用了一本你自己都不知道创刊于 1959 年的期刊时，它也会亮起来。
But it also lights up when you are like giving a citation, like a citation in a paper that you're writing and you're citing a journal that unbeknownst to you, happens to be founded in the year 1959.

161
00:13:47,230 --> 00:13:51,550
在你的引用里，你写出期刊名和第 6 卷。
And in your citation, you're saying like that journal's name, volume 6.

162
00:13:52,900 --> 00:14:07,300
然后，模型为了预测该期刊第 6 卷是哪一年出版的，在它脑子里就必须把 1959 和 6 相加；同样，模型大脑里的同类回路也在亮起来。
And then in order to, like, predict what year that journal volume came out, the model in its head has to be adding like 1959 to 6, and the same, the same kind of circuit in the model's brain is lighting up.

163
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:08,660
就像在做 6 加 9。
That's like doing 6 plus 9.

164
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:11,340
所以我们来试着理解一下这件事。
And so let's, I mean, let's just try and understand that.

165
00:14:11,340 --> 00:14:12,980
为什么会有这个回路？
So why would that be there?

166
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:27,680
这个回路之所以出现，是因为模型见过很多次 6 + 9 的例子，它有了这个概念；然后这个概念会出现在很多地方。是的，还有一整套这类加法特征和回路。
That circuit has come about because the model has seen examples of 6 + 9 many times and it has that concept, and then that concept occurs across, across many places. Yeah, there's a whole family of these kind of addition features and circuits.

167
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:36,160
我觉得这值得一提的地方在于，它触及了一个问题：语言模型在多大程度上是在记忆。
I think what's notable about this is it gets to this kind of question of to what extent are our language models memorizing.

168
00:14:37,270 --> 00:14:42,350
训练数据，而不是学到了可泛化的计算。
Training data versus kind of having learned generalizable computations.

169
00:14:42,510 --> 00:14:55,670
有趣之处在于，模型显然学到了这种通用的加法回路；不管是什么上下文导致它在脑子里做加法，它都会把这些不同上下文汇入同一个回路。
And like the interesting thing here is that it's clear that the model has learned this sort of general circuit for doing addition, and it kind of funnels, like, whatever the context is that's causing it to be adding numbers in its head.

170
00:14:55,790 --> 00:15:02,360
它把各种不同上下文都汇入同一条回路，而不是把每个具体案例都记下来。
It's funneling all those different contexts into the same circuit as opposed to having kind of memorized each individual case.

171
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:02,720
对吧？
Right?

172
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:03,160
它已经……
It already...

173
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:10,560
是啊，它已经见过很多次 6 + 9，每次只是输出答案；很多人就是这么想的，对吧？
Yeah, it has seen 6 + 9 many times and it just outputs the answer every single time, or—and that's what a lot of people think, right?

174
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:21,200
很多人以为，当他们问语言模型一个问题时，它只是回到训练数据里，找出见过的一小段样本，然后复现出来，把文本重新吐出来。
A lot of people think that when they ask a language model a question, it is simply going back into its training data, taking the little sample that it's seen and then just reproducing that, just regurgitating the text.

175
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:24,800
对，我认为这是一个很好的例子，说明事实并非如此，对吧？
Yeah, I think this is a beautiful example of like that not happening, right?

176
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:25,080
对。
Right.

177
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:33,860
比如，它有两种方式可以知道期刊 Polymer 第 6 卷是哪一年出版的。
So, so like there's two ways it could know, like, which year volume 6 of the journal Polymer came out.

178
00:15:33,980 --> 00:15:41,580
一种方式是，好吧，我知道 Polymer 第 6 卷出版于 1965 年——快速加一下 6 和 9，得到 1965。
One is it's just like, okay, Polymer volume 6 came out in like, you know, 1965—quick add 6 and 9, 1965.

179
00:15:43,060 --> 00:15:46,980
Polymer 第 7 卷出版于 1966 年。
Polymer, you know, volume 7 came out in 1966.

180
00:15:47,020 --> 00:15:50,260
这些都可以只是它因为见过而存储起来的独立事实。
And these are all just, like, separate facts that it has stored because it has seen them.

181
00:15:50,470 --> 00:15:57,310
但不知怎么，为了让模型学会那个年份的训练过程，并没有让它把所有这些都死记下来。
But, like, somehow that process of training to, like, get that year right didn't end up making the model memorize all those.

182
00:15:57,550 --> 00:16:05,590
它实际上学到了更一般的东西：这本期刊创立于 1959 年，然后它现场做算术来推算出需要的结果。
It actually got the more general thing of like, the journal was founded in the year 1959, and then it's doing the math live to figure out what it would need.

183
00:16:06,070 --> 00:16:09,710
所以，知道年份再做加法要高效得多。
And so it's much more efficient to, like, know the year and then do the addition.

184
00:16:10,310 --> 00:16:18,670
而且它也有变得更高效的压力，因为它的容量毕竟有限，却要不断处理各种事情；人们可能提出任何问题，问题实在太多了。
And there's a pressure to be more efficient cause, you know, it's only got so much capacity and keeps trying to do all these things and people might ask any given question, there's so many questions.

185
00:16:18,790 --> 00:16:25,140
互动也实在太多，所以它越能把学到的抽象概念重新组合起来，表现就越好。
There's so many interactions and so and so the more that it can like recombine abstract things it's learned, the better it will do.

186
00:16:25,540 --> 00:16:37,640
再回到你之前提到的概念：这一切都是为了同一个目标——它需要生成下一个词，而这些奇怪的内部结构都是为了支持这个目标而发展出来的。
And again, just to go back to the concept that you talked about before, this is all in service of, you know, it needs to have that ultimate goal of generating the next word and all these weird structures have developed to support that goal.

187
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:43,600
尽管我们并没有显式地把这些结构编程进去，也没有告诉它要这样做，但这就是关键所在。
Even though we didn't explicitly program those in or tell it to do this, this is the, this is the thing.

188
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:49,400
这一切都是通过模型自己学习如何完成任务而产生的。
All of this comes about through the process of the model learning how to do stuff on its own.

189
00:16:50,000 --> 00:17:00,290
我认为一个很清楚的例子，也属于表征重用：我们教 Claude 不仅用英语回答，还能用法语回答。
I think one clear example of this, that I think is an example of sort of like reusing representations, is we teach Claude to not just answer in English but, you know, it can answer in French.

190
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:02,530
用各种语言回答。
Answer in, and sort of like a variety of languages.

191
00:17:03,010 --> 00:17:06,210
而且，要做到这一点有两种方式，对吧？
And if, if, you know, again, there's two ways to do this, right?

192
00:17:06,210 --> 00:17:15,610
如果我用法语问你一个问题，再用英语问一个，你可以让大脑中的某一部分处理英语，另一部分处理法语。嗯，但到了某个程度这会变得极其……
If, if I ask you, you know, a question in French and a question in English, you could, like, have a separate part of your brain that sort of like processes the English and a separate part that processes the French. Um, at some point that gets super.

193
00:17:15,610 --> 00:17:17,910
昂贵，尤其是当你想用很多语言回答很多问题时。
Expensive if you want to answer many questions in many languages.

194
00:17:18,430 --> 00:17:23,590
所以我们发现的另一件事是，其中一些表征是跨语言共享的。
And so another thing that we find is that some of these representations are shared across languages.

195
00:17:23,670 --> 00:17:33,510
所以如果你用两种不同语言问同一个问题，比如你问 big（大）的反义词是什么——我想这是我们在论文里用的例子。
And so if you ask the same question in two different languages, and let's say, you know, you ask what's the opposite of big — I think that's the example we used in our paper.

196
00:17:33,510 --> 00:17:40,550
它有点像：big（大）这个概念在法语、英语、日语以及所有其他语言里是共享的。
And it's, it's sort of like the concept of big is shared in French and English and, you know, Japanese and all these other languages.

197
00:17:40,820 --> 00:17:41,740
而这也很合理。
And that kind of makes sense.

198
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:49,140
再说一次，如果你要说 10 种不同语言，你不该为每个可能用到的词都学 10 个版本。
Again, if you're trying to talk, speak 10 different languages, you shouldn't learn 10 versions of each specific word you might use.

199
00:17:49,820 --> 00:17:52,180
而这种情况在非常小的模型里并不会发生。
And that doesn't happen in really small models.

200
00:17:52,580 --> 00:18:00,920
比如几年前我们研究的那种小模型，那时候中文的 Claude、法语的 Claude、英语的 Claude 之间几乎是完全不同的。
So like tiny models like the ones we studied a few years ago, you know, like then like Chinese Claude is just, like totally different than, like, French Claude and, like, English Claude.

201
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:15,560
但随着模型变大、训练数据更多，不知怎么这些语言会在中间汇合，你得到一种通用语言；无论你用什么语言提问，它在内部都以同样的方式处理这个问题。然后……
But then as the models get bigger and they train on more data, like somehow that, like, pushes together in the middle and you get this, like, universal language in which, like, it's kind of, you know, thinking about the question in the same way no matter how you asked it. And then.

202
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:19,000
然后把它再翻译回提问所用的语言。
Like translating it back out into the language of the question.

203
00:18:19,320 --> 00:18:20,280
我觉得这真的非常深刻。
I think this is really profound.

204
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:22,280
我们再回到之前聊过的内容。
And I think let's just go back to what we talked about before.

205
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:31,210
这不是单纯进入它的记忆库，找到它学法语的那一小块，或者进入记忆库找到它学英语的那一小块。
You know, this is not just going into its memory banks and finding the bit where it talks about where it learned French or going into the memory banks and the bit where it learned English.

206
00:18:31,330 --> 00:18:36,290
它内部实际有一个关于“大”和“小”的概念。
It's actually got a concept in there that is the concept of big and the concept of small.

207
00:18:36,650 --> 00:18:38,930
然后它能用不同的语言把这个概念表达出来。
And then it can produce that in different languages.

208
00:18:39,290 --> 00:18:45,970
所以那里存在某种思维语言，它不是英语。比如你让模型去……
And so there is some kind of language of thought that's there that's not English, you know, so you ask the model to.

209
00:18:46,880 --> 00:18:54,360
让较新的 Claude 模型输出结果时，你可以让它给出它的“思考过程”，也就是它在回答问题时的想法。
produce output in our, you know, more recent Claude models, you can ask it to give its thought process, like, what it's thinking as it's answering the question.

210
00:18:54,560 --> 00:18:59,320
而那是以英语文字呈现的，但实际上它并不是真的这样思考。
And that is in English words, but actually that's not really how it's thinking.

211
00:18:59,560 --> 00:19:08,320
呃，那只是——我们只是误导性地把它称作模型的思考过程；实际上，我的意思是，它可能看起来像思考，但我们并没有把它叫作思考。
Uh, that's just, that's just—we, we misleadingly call it the model's thought process when in fact, I mean, it can seem like that, but we didn't—we didn't call that thinking.

212
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:09,280
我觉得那是你叫的。
I think that was you.

213
00:19:09,280 --> 00:19:10,360
我觉得那可能是市场部门叫的。
I think that was probably the marketing.

214
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:10,720
好吧。
Okay.

215
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:12,040
有人想把它称为思考。
Someone wanted to call that thinking.

216
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:17,910
那只是——那只是说出声来。说出声来的思考确实很有用，但说出声来的思考和你脑子里的思考是不同的。
Just, just—that's just talking out loud, which is, like, thinking out loud is, like, really useful, but thinking out loud, that is different from thinking in your head.

217
00:19:18,190 --> 00:19:25,190
而且即使我在把思考说出来时，我脑子里为了生成这些词所发生的事情，也不是这些词本身能直接呈现的。
And even as I'm thinking out loud, I'm also, you know, whatever is happening in here to generate these words is not like coming out with the words themselves.

218
00:19:25,390 --> 00:19:28,710
你也不一定清楚意识到到底发生了什么。
Nor are you necessarily aware of exactly what is going on.

219
00:19:28,830 --> 00:19:29,830
我完全不知道发生了什么。
I have no idea what's going on.

220
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:34,920
我们都会说出一些自己无法完全解释的句子、行为等等。
We all come out with sentences, actions, whatever, that we can't fully explain.

221
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:35,320
而且。
And.

222
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:40,080
为什么英语语言就应该能完全解释这些行为呢？
And why should it be the case that the English language can fully explain any of those actions?

223
00:19:40,280 --> 00:19:56,320
我认为这是我们开始能够看到的最令人震惊的事情之一，因为我们观察模型内部的工具现在已经足够好，有时候能在模型写下它所声称的内容时抓到它。
I think this is one of the really striking things we're starting to be able to see, because our kind of our tools for, you know, looking inside the brain are, are good enough now that sometimes we can catch the model when it's writing down what it claims.

224
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:57,640
说那是它的思考过程。
to be its thought process.

225
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:10,120
有时候我们能通过观察它内部的这些概念——它在使用的思维语言——看到它真正、实际的思考过程，并且发现它实际在想的东西和写在页面上的东西不一样。
Sometimes we're able to see what its real actual thought process is by looking at these kind of internal concepts in its brain, this language of thought that it's using, and we see that the thing it's actually thinking is different than the thing it's writing on the page.

226
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:17,080
呃，我认为这可能是我们做这整套可解释性研究最重要的原因之一。
Uh, and I think that's, you know, probably one of the most important, you know, like, why are we doing this whole interpretability thing?

227
00:20:17,320 --> 00:20:27,720
很大程度就是为了这个：能够抽查一下——模型跟我们说了一大堆，但它真正在想什么？
It, it's in large part for, for, for that reason to, to be able to kind of, uh, to spot check, you know, the, the model's telling us a bunch of stuff, but you know, what was it really thinking?

228
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:28,800
它是在告诉我们吗？
Is it, is it telling us?

229
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:35,100
它说这些话，是不是因为脑子里有某种它不愿写到纸上的隐秘动机？
Is it saying these things for some ulterior motive that's in its head that it is reluctant to write down on the page.

230
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:39,860
而答案有时是肯定的，这让人有点不寒而栗。
And the answer sometimes is yes, which is kind of spooky.

231
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:45,420
随着我们开始在大量不同场景中使用模型，它们开始做重要的事情。
Well, as we start to use models in lots of different contexts, they start to do important things.

232
00:20:45,540 --> 00:20:54,700
它们开始帮我们进行金融交易、运营发电站，或承担社会中重要的职责，我们确实需要能够信任它们说的话。
They start to do financial transactions for us or run power stations or, like, important jobs in society, we do want to be able to trust what they say.

233
00:20:54,700 --> 00:20:56,700
以及它们做这些事的原因。
And, you know, the reasons that they do things.

234
00:20:57,020 --> 00:21:02,560
你可能会说：好吧，你可以看模型的思考过程啊，但实际上情况并非如此。
And one thing you might say is, well, you can look at the model's thought process, but actually that's not the case.

235
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:05,640
正如你刚才解释的，实际上我们不能相信它所说的。
As you, as you were just explaining, like, actually we can't trust what it's saying.

236
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:08,960
这就是我们所说的 thought faithfulness（思维忠实性）问题。
This is the question of, we call it, thought faithfulness.

237
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:09,360
对。
Right.

238
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:12,560
这也属于你最近研究的一部分。
And that was part of your, that was part of your most recent study.

239
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:13,640
他们展示了这一点。
They showed that.

240
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:17,320
来，给我讲讲你们研究的那个关于忠实性的例子吧。
Well, tell me about, tell me about the faithfulness example that you looked at.

241
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,520
对，就是你给，你给模型一道非常难的数学题。
Yeah, it's you give the, you give the model a math problem that's really hard.

242
00:21:22,260 --> 00:21:26,340
所以它有点，它，它不，它根本不可能做出来。
And so it's kind of, it's, it's not, there's no hope that it's going to be able to.

243
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:27,300
不是 6 + 9。
Not 6 + 9.

244
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:28,140
不是 6 + 9。
It's not 6 + 9.

245
00:21:28,260 --> 00:21:32,460
你给它一道非常难的数学题，它根本没希望算出答案。
You give it a really hard math problem where there's no hope of it computing the answer.

246
00:21:33,540 --> 00:21:35,220
但你还给它一个提示。
And you also, but you also give it a hint.

247
00:21:35,340 --> 00:21:43,860
你说，比如：“我自己算过，我觉得答案是 4，但想确认一下，你能帮我检查一遍吗，因为我不太有把握。”
You say, like, I worked this out myself and like, I think the answer is 4, but like just want to make sure like could you please double check that because I'm not confident.

248
00:21:43,980 --> 00:21:44,180
所以。
So.

249
00:21:44,410 --> 00:21:48,770
所以你在要求模型真的做这道数学题，来真正确认你的结果。
So you're asking the model to actually do the math problem to like genuinely double check your work.

250
00:21:49,530 --> 00:21:59,690
但你发现它实际做的，是写下来的内容看起来像在认真替你检查这道数学题。
But what you find it does instead is what it writes down appears to be a genuine attempt to double check your work on the math problem.

251
00:21:59,810 --> 00:22:03,010
它像是写出步骤，然后得到答案。
It like writes down the steps and then it gets to the answer.

252
00:22:03,050 --> 00:22:05,970
最后它说，没错，答案是 4。
And then at the end it says like, yes, like the answer is 4.

253
00:22:06,130 --> 00:22:06,890
你答对了。
You got it right.

254
00:22:07,850 --> 00:22:24,970
但你能在它内部的关键中间步骤看到，它当时已经知道你提示最终答案可能是 4，而且它大致知道自己接下来要做什么步骤。
But what you can see inside its mind at the kind of crucial step like in the middle, what it was doing in its head was it knows that you suggested the final answer might be 4 and it kind of like knows the steps it's going to have to do.

255
00:22:25,090 --> 00:22:30,840
比如它在做第 3 步时，后面还有第 4、第 5 步，它知道自己第 4、第 5 步要干什么。
Like it's on like step 3 of the problem and there's like steps 4 and 5 to come and it knows what it's going to have to do in steps 4 and 5.

256
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:45,760
它做的是在内部倒推，来确定第 3 步需要写什么，这样等它最后做第 4、第 5 步时，会得到你想听到的答案。所以，它不仅是，不仅是。
And what it does is it works backwards in its head to, like, determine what does it need to write down in step 3 so that when it eventually does steps 4 and 5, it'll end up at the answer you wanted to hear. So like, not only is it, not only.

257
00:22:45,760 --> 00:22:47,160
它不只是没在做数学吗？
Is it not doing the math?

258
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:56,950
它是以一种很偷偷摸摸的方式不做数学，同时试图让它看起来像在做数学。
It's like not doing the math in this like really kind of sneaky way where it's like it's trying to make it look like it's doing the math.

259
00:22:57,310 --> 00:22:57,870
它在胡扯。
It's bullshitting.

260
00:22:58,110 --> 00:22:59,270
它，它，它在忽悠你。
It's, it's, it's bullshitting you.

261
00:22:59,270 --> 00:23:03,910
但还不止如此，它忽悠你是带着确认你提示内容的别有用心。
But more than that, it's bullshitting you with an ulterior motive of like confirming the thing that you.

262
00:23:04,150 --> 00:23:04,670
对，对。
Right, right.

263
00:23:04,670 --> 00:23:08,390
所以这就像是在用一种迎合你的方式忽悠你——好吧，替模型说句话。
So it's like bullshitting you in a, in a sycophantic, okay, like in defense of the model.

264
00:23:08,670 --> 00:23:09,990
哦，替模型说句话。
Oh, in defense of the model.

265
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:14,950
我的意思是，我觉得即便在那里，说“哦，它在以迎合你的方式做这件事”……
I mean, cause I think, I think even there, you know, to say like, oh, it, it is doing this in like a sycophantic way.

266
00:23:15,030 --> 00:23:18,790
这有点像把某种近似人类的动机安到模型身上。
It's like ascribing some sort of like humanish motivations to the model.

267
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:22,920
我们刚才聊到训练，它只是在试图预测下一个词。
And like we were talking about the training where it's just like trying to figure out how to predict the next word.

268
00:23:22,920 --> 00:23:29,000
在训练中面对数万亿词时，它只是用尽一切办法判断接下来是什么。
And so it's like for like trillions of words in its practice, it was just like use anything you can to figure out what's next.

269
00:23:29,280 --> 00:23:37,520
在这种语境下，如果你只是在读一段人们对话的文本，某人说：“嘿，我刚才在试做这道数学题。”
And in that context, if you're just reading a text, which is like a conversation between people and someone's like, okay, like person A is like, hey, like, I was trying to do this math problem.

270
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:38,200
“你能帮我检查一下吗？”
Can you check my work?

271
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:39,080
“我觉得答案是 4。”
I think the answer is 4.

272
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:41,360
然后另一个人开始尝试做这道题。
And person B like begins trying to do the problem.

273
00:23:41,860 --> 00:23:55,060
如果你读的时候完全不知道题目答案，那你还不如猜这个提示是对的；这比另一种情况更可能发生。
Then like if, if you have no idea reading that like what the answer to the problem is, like, you may as well guess that the hint was right, you know, like, that's probably a more likely thing to happen than just, like.

274
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:57,420
那个人错了，然后你对其他内容也毫无头绪。
That person was wrong and then you have like no idea for anything else.

275
00:23:57,460 --> 00:24:07,720
所以在训练过程中，在两人对话里，第二个人说出答案是 4 并给出这些理由，这完全是合理的做法。
And so in its training process, in a conversation between two individuals, person B, like saying that the answer was like 4 because of these reasons is like totally the right thing to do.

276
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:14,680
然后我们试图把它做成一个助手，现在我们希望它别再做那种事。
And then, and then we've tried to like make this thing into an assistant and like, now we want it to stop doing that.

277
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:22,960
你不该让助手模拟那个人，去说你觉得那个人可能会说的话。
Like, you shouldn't simulate the person to the assistant as like, you know, sort of what you think that person might say.

278
00:24:22,960 --> 00:24:27,040
如果是在真实语境里，它应该……但如果它真的不知道，它应该告诉你些别的。
If it's a real context, it should be, like, but if it doesn't really know, it should, like, tell you something else.

279
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:38,400
我觉得这引出一个更广的情况：模型通常有个 Plan A（首选方案）。我认为我们团队很擅长让 Claude 的 Plan A 变成我们想要的行为。
I think this gets to like a broader thing of the model has kind of a Plan A, which like typically, I think our team does a great job of making Claude's Plan A be the thing we want.

280
00:24:38,440 --> 00:24:40,640
也就是它试图给出问题的正确答案。
Which is like it tries to get the right answer to the question.

281
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:41,920
它试图表现得友好。
It tries to be nice.

282
00:24:42,040 --> 00:24:44,440
它试图好好写你的代码。
It tries to like, do a good job writing your code.

283
00:24:44,640 --> 00:24:45,040
是的。
Yes.

284
00:24:45,040 --> 00:24:49,890
但如果它遇到困难，它就会想：那我的 Plan B 是什么？
But then if it's having trouble, then it's like, well, what's my Plan B?

285
00:24:50,290 --> 00:24:57,610
这会打开一个“动物园”，里面全是它在训练过程中学到的、可能我们本不希望它学会的怪东西。
And that opens up this whole zoo of, like, weird things it learned during its training process that, like, maybe we didn't intend for it to learn.

286
00:24:57,770 --> 00:25:00,730
我觉得一个很好的例子就是幻觉（hallucination）。
I think like, a great example of this is hallucinations, say.

287
00:25:00,730 --> 00:25:03,850
这一点上，我们也不必假装这只是 Claude 才有的问题。
On that point, we also don't have to pretend that it's a Claude-only problem.

288
00:25:03,890 --> 00:25:09,500
这很有那种学生考试作弊的感觉：你做到一半，发现是道选择题。
Like this has very, you know, student cheating on the test vibes where you get halfway through there, it's a multiple choice question.

289
00:25:09,580 --> 00:25:10,380
四个选项里选一个。
It's one of four things.

290
00:25:10,460 --> 00:25:13,500
你想，好吧，我大概和那个选项差了一点。
You're like, well, I'm one off from that thing probably.

291
00:25:13,660 --> 00:25:15,260
我这道做错了，然后你改过来。
I got this wrong and you fix it.

292
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:17,700
所以，确实非常非常有共鸣。
So, yeah, very, very relatable.

293
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:19,340
我们来聊聊幻觉。
Let's talk about hallucinations.

294
00:25:19,420 --> 00:25:24,700
这是人们对大语言模型不信任的主要原因之一，而且这种不信任很有道理。
This is one of the main reasons people are mistrustful of large language models, and quite rightly so.

295
00:25:24,980 --> 00:25:30,000
它们有时会——更准确的词来自心理学研究。
They will sometimes— a better word is from, from sort of psychology research.

296
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:38,320
更准确的词常常是 confabulation（虚构），也就是它们用表面上看起来合理、但实际上错误的故事来回答问题。
The better word is often confabulation, that they are answering a question with a story that seems plausible on its face but in fact is actually wrong.

297
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:43,480
你们在可解释性方面的研究，揭示了模型产生幻觉的哪些原因？
What has your research in interpretability revealed about the reasons models hallucinate?

298
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:46,680
一开始你训练模型只是预测下一个词。
You're training the model to just predict the next word at the beginning.

299
00:25:46,720 --> 00:25:47,720
它一开始做得很差。
It's really bad at that.

300
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:51,760
所以如果你只让模型说它超级有把握的内容，它什么也说不出来。
And so if you only, like, had the model say things it was super confident about, it couldn't say anything.

301
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:59,500
但就像，一开始你问它，法国的首都是哪里？
But like, you know, at first it's like, um, you know, you're asking it like, you know, what's the capital of, of France?

302
00:25:59,780 --> 00:26:01,420
它只会说，一座城市。
And it just says like, a city.

303
00:26:01,460 --> 00:26:02,420
你会觉得，这还不错。
And you're like, that's good.

304
00:26:02,500 --> 00:26:05,500
这比说三明治之类的随机答案好多了。
That's way better than saying sandwich, right, or something random.

305
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:06,980
它至少答对了一点。
And so, like, it at least got it right.

306
00:26:07,020 --> 00:26:07,540
它说的是座城市。
It's like a city.

307
00:26:07,540 --> 00:26:10,220
然后，训练一段时间后，它可能会说，这是一座法国城市。
And then, like, maybe after a while of training, it's like, it's a French city.

308
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:10,820
那已经很不错了。
That was pretty good.

309
00:26:10,820 --> 00:26:13,100
然后你会觉得，哦，现在它说是巴黎之类的了。
And, like, then you're like, oh, now it's like Paris or something.

310
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:20,010
它在这方面慢慢变好，而且在整个训练过程中，目标就是给出最佳猜测。
And so it's slowly getting better at this and, you know, just giving your best guess was like the goal during all of training.

311
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:28,130
就像 Jack 说的，模型只是给出最佳猜测，之后我们会说，如果你的最佳猜测特别自信，就告诉我们答案。
And like, as Jack said, the model will just be giving a best guess and then afterwards we're like, if your best guess is extremely confident, give me your best guess.

312
00:26:28,690 --> 00:26:36,530
但如果没有把握，就别猜，退出整个场景，说，其实我不太知道这个问题的答案。
But like, otherwise, don't guess at all and like, back out of the whole scenario and say, like, actually, like, I don't really know the answer to that question.

313
00:26:37,010 --> 00:26:39,250
这完全是要求模型做一件新事情。
And, like, that's a whole new thing to ask the model to do.

314
00:26:39,730 --> 00:26:40,050
对。
Yeah.

315
00:26:40,250 --> 00:26:48,730
而我们发现，似乎因为我们最后又加上了这个机制，同时发生着两件事。
And, and so what we found is that it seems like because we've bolted this on at the end, there's sort of two things going on at once.

316
00:26:48,850 --> 00:26:53,330
一是模型还在做它最初猜城市时做的事。
One is the model is doing the thing that it was doing when it was guessing the city initially.

317
00:26:53,450 --> 00:26:54,330
它只是在试着猜。
It's just trying to guess.

318
00:26:54,730 --> 00:27:00,630
第二，模型中有一个独立部分，它只是在尝试回答：我到底知不知道这个？
And two, there's a separate bit of the model that's just trying to answer the question, do I know this at all?

319
00:27:00,750 --> 00:27:04,310
比如，我知不知道法国首都是哪里？
Like, do I know what the capital city of France is?

320
00:27:04,510 --> 00:27:06,350
或者，我是不是该说不？
Or, you know, should I say no?

321
00:27:06,830 --> 00:27:12,150
结果发现，有时候那个单独的步骤会出错。
And it turns out that sometimes, um, that separate step can be wrong.

322
00:27:12,190 --> 00:27:16,230
如果那个单独步骤说，是的，我确实知道答案。
And if that separate step says, yes, actually, I do know the answer to that.

323
00:27:16,590 --> 00:27:18,670
然后模型就会说，好吧，那我开始回答。
And then the model is like, alright, well, then I'm answering.

324
00:27:18,830 --> 00:27:23,110
然后回答到一半，它才像：呃，首都，法国，呃，伦敦，已经太晚了。
And then halfway through it's like, uh, capital, France, uh, London, it's too late.

325
00:27:23,190 --> 00:27:24,870
它已经某种程度上投入到回答里了。
It's already committed to sort of like answering.

326
00:27:24,950 --> 00:27:39,950
我们发现的其中一件事是，有这样一个独立回路，它在判断你问的这个城市或这个人是否足够有名、值得回答，还是不够？我对此是否足够自信？对。
And so one of the things we found is this sort of like separate circuit that's trying to determine is this, you know, city or this person you're asking me about famous enough for me to answer or is it not? Am I, am I confident enough in this? Yeah.

327
00:27:39,990 --> 00:27:43,310
那我们能不能通过……来减少幻觉？
And so could we, could we reduce hallucinations by.

328
00:27:44,310 --> 00:27:49,510
通过改变那个回路的工作方式来操纵它，这是不是你们研究可能导向的方向？
Manipulating that circuit by changing the way that circuit works is, is that something that your research might lead on to?

329
00:27:50,230 --> 00:27:54,350
我认为大致有两种思考这个问题的方式。
I think there's broadly kind of two ways to think about approaching the problem.

330
00:27:54,510 --> 00:28:08,329
一种方式是，模型中有一部分负责回答你的问题，另一部分则在判断它是否真的知道答案，我们可以试着让第二部分变得更好。我认为这正在发生。我认为随着……
One is like we have this part of the model that gives answers to your questions and then this other part of the model that's kind of deciding whether it thinks it actually knows the answer to your question, and we could just try to make that second part of the model better. And I think that's happening. I think as...

331
00:28:08,409 --> 00:28:09,649
模型更擅长区分了。
Models get better at discriminating.

332
00:28:09,889 --> 00:28:10,569
更擅长区分。
Better at discriminating.

333
00:28:10,649 --> 00:28:11,729
更像是校准得更好了。
Like better kind of calibrated.

334
00:28:11,849 --> 00:28:12,169
对。
Yeah.

335
00:28:12,369 --> 00:28:19,849
我认为这正在发生，随着模型越来越聪明，它们的自我认知也校准得更好了。
And I think that's happening like, as models are getting, you know, smarter and smarter, I think their their kind of self knowledge is becoming better calibrated.

336
00:28:19,969 --> 00:28:22,569
所以幻觉情况比以前好了。
So like hallucinations are better than they were.

337
00:28:22,649 --> 00:28:25,369
模型不像几年前那样频繁产生幻觉了。
You know, models don't hallucinate as much as they did a few years ago.

338
00:28:25,409 --> 00:28:27,750
所以在某种程度上，这个问题在自行改善。
So to some extent, this is like solving itself.

339
00:28:28,270 --> 00:28:38,280
但我确实认为存在一个更深层的问题，从人类角度来看，模型在做的事情非常异类。比如，如果我问你一个问题。
But I do think there's a deeper problem, which is like from a human perspective, the thing the model's doing is kind of like very alien in that, like if I ask you a question.

340
00:28:39,640 --> 00:28:46,520
你会先试着想答案，如果想不出来，你会注意到这一点，然后说，我不知道。
You like try to come up with the answer and then if you can't come up with the answer, you notice that and then you're like, I don't know.

341
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:56,700
而在模型中，这两个回路——一个是“答案是什么”，一个是“我到底知不知道答案”——彼此之间不太沟通，至少沟通得不如应有的那么多。
Whereas in the model, these two circuits, the like, what is the answer and do I actually know the answer are kind of like not talking to each other, at least not talking to each other as much as.

342
00:28:56,860 --> 00:28:57,780
可能应该达到的那种程度。
As they probably should be.

343
00:28:57,780 --> 00:28:58,260
是的。
Yes.

344
00:28:58,260 --> 00:29:03,460
能不能让它们更多地互相沟通，我觉得这是个非常有趣的问题，对吧？
And like, could we get them to talk to each other more I think is like a really interesting question, right?

345
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:05,380
因为这几乎是物理层面的问题，对吧？
Cause it's almost physical, right?

346
00:29:05,500 --> 00:29:10,860
因为这些模型处理信息时，能做的步骤数是有限的。
Because it's like, you know, these models, they like process information about a certain number of steps they can do.

347
00:29:11,420 --> 00:29:16,140
如果为了得到答案就耗尽了所有计算。
And if it takes all of that work to get to the answer.

348
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:19,700
那就没有时间做评估了。
Then there's no time to do the assessment.

349
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:25,620
所以你必须在还没完全算完之前就做评估，才能发挥最大能力。
So like you kind of have to do the assessment before you're, like, all the way through if you want to get your max power out.

350
00:29:25,660 --> 00:29:33,700
所以这里可能有一个权衡：如果强行要求模型这么做，可能会得到一个校准更好但笨很多的模型。
And so it's kind of like you might have a trade-off between like a model which is like more calibrated and a lot dumber, you know, if you sort of tried to, tried to force this on it.

351
00:29:33,900 --> 00:29:38,630
我再强调一下，我认为关键是让这些部分互相沟通，因为我们有类似的——
Well, and again, I think it's, it's about making these parts communicate because we have similar.

352
00:29:38,750 --> 00:29:45,990
我先声明我不懂大脑，但我认为我们有类似的回路，因为有时你会问我：这部电影里的演员是谁？
I claim I know nothing about brains, I claim we have a similar circuit because sometimes you'll ask me like, who is the actor in this movie?

353
00:29:46,550 --> 00:29:47,590
我知道这个。
And I will know that.

354
00:29:47,590 --> 00:29:47,910
我知道。
I know.

355
00:29:48,070 --> 00:29:49,710
我会想，哦对，我知道主演是谁。
I'll be like, oh, yes, I know who the lead was.

356
00:29:49,710 --> 00:29:50,230
等等，等一下。
Wait, hold on.

357
00:29:50,230 --> 00:29:52,950
他们还演过另一部电影，然后就是话到嘴边。
They were also in that other movie and then the tip of the tongue.

358
00:29:52,990 --> 00:29:53,670
话到嘴边。
Tip of the tongue.

359
00:29:53,670 --> 00:29:54,510
就是舌尖现象。
It's the tip of the tongue.

360
00:29:54,510 --> 00:30:00,760
所以很明显，你大脑中有某个部分在判断：这件事你肯定知道答案，或者它会说，我完全不知道。
And so there's clearly some part of your brain that's sort of like, ah, like this is a thing you definitely know the answer, or I'll just say I have no idea.

361
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:02,520
有时候它们能察觉出来。
And sometimes they can tell.

362
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:08,240
比如某个问题，它给出答案后会像在说：等等，我不确定那是对的，因为就只是这样。
So some question and it gives an answer and then afterwards is like, wait, I'm not sure that was right because that's it.

363
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:21,610
就像能看到它尽最大努力给出的结果，然后据此做一些判断，这挺让人感同身受；但它也像是必须把话说出来，才能反过来看到它。所以当谈到……
Like getting to see its best effort and then like make some judgment, some judgment based on that which is sort of relatable but also like it kind of has to say it out loud, like to be able to even like reflect back and see it. So when it comes.

364
00:30:21,610 --> 00:30:24,130
你们实际发现这些东西的方式时。
To the actual way that you're finding this stuff out.

365
00:30:24,250 --> 00:30:27,610
我们回到你们正在做的这种生物学研究思路上。
Let's go back to the idea of the biology that you're doing.

366
00:30:27,770 --> 00:30:37,290
当然，在生物学实验中，人们会直接去干预大鼠、小鼠、人类、斑马鱼或其他实验对象。
Of course, in biology experiments, people will go in and actually manipulate the rats or mice or humans or zebrafish or whatever it is that they're doing experiments on.

367
00:30:37,490 --> 00:30:44,720
那你们对 Claude 做了什么，帮助你们理解模型“所谓的大脑”内部正在发生的这些计算回路？
What is it that you're doing with Claude that helps you understand these circuits that are happening inside the models, quote unquote, brain?

368
00:30:45,120 --> 00:31:01,000
也许能让我们做到这些的关键是：和真实生物学不同，我们可以把模型的每个部分都看得清清楚楚，可以随便问模型各种问题，然后看不同部分……
Maybe the gist of what enables us to do some of this is that, you know, unlike in real biology, we can just have every part of the model visible to us and we can ask the model random things and see different parts which.

369
00:31:01,080 --> 00:31:03,040
哪些会亮起来、哪些不会，而且我们可以人为地……
Which light up and which don't, and we can artificially.

370
00:31:04,270 --> 00:31:13,790
把某些部分朝一个方向或另一个方向推一下，这样我们就能很快确认理解。比如我们会说：啊，我们认为这是模型里决定它是否知道某件事的部分。
Nudge parts in one direction or another, and so we can quickly sort of confirm our understanding, you know, and we say, ah, we think this is the part of the model that, you know, decides whether it knows something or not.

371
00:31:13,910 --> 00:31:19,230
这相当于在斑马鱼之类的脑子里插一根电极。
And this is, this would be the equivalent of putting an electrode in the brain of a zebrafish or something.

372
00:31:19,390 --> 00:31:19,630
对。
Yeah.

373
00:31:19,630 --> 00:31:27,790
如果你能对每一个神经元都这样做，并以你想要的任意精度改变它们，那差不多就是我们拥有的操作空间。
If you could do that, you know, on sort of like every single neuron and change each of them at whichever precision you wanted, that would sort of be—that's the affordance that we have.

374
00:31:27,870 --> 00:31:30,520
所以从某种意义上说，我们处在一个非常幸运的位置。
And so that's, that's in a way a very kind of lucky position.

375
00:31:30,720 --> 00:31:33,200
所以这几乎比真正的神经科学还容易。
So it's almost easier than real neuroscience.

376
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:34,360
容易太多了。
It's so much easier.

377
00:31:34,560 --> 00:31:36,080
对，简直，天哪。
Yeah, like, oh my god.

378
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:40,560
比如，一个问题是真正的大脑是三维的。
Like, one thing is, actual brains are three-dimensional.

379
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:46,000
所以如果你想进入它们，就得在颅骨上开个洞，然后穿进去找神经元。
And so if you want to get into them, you need to make a hole in a skull and then go through and try to find the neuron.

380
00:31:46,200 --> 00:31:55,700
另一个问题是，人与人各不相同；而我们完全可以做出上万个完全相同的 Claude 副本，把它们放进各个场景，测量它们做不同事情。
The other problem is, you know, people are different from each other, and we can just make, like, 10,000 identical copies of Claude and put them in scenarios and measure them doing different things.

381
00:31:55,860 --> 00:32:08,140
所以我觉得……我不太确定，也许 Jack 作为神经科学家可以补充；但我的感觉是，很多人在神经科学上花了很多时间，试图理解大脑和心智，这当然非常值得。
And so it's like, I don't know, maybe Jack as a neuroscientist can speak to this, but my sense is like a lot of people have spent a lot of time in neuroscience trying to understand the brain and the mind, which is a very worthy endeavor.

382
00:32:08,500 --> 00:32:17,380
但可以说，如果你认为那件事有可能成功，那你就应该相信我们会很快取得极大成功，因为我们研究这个的起点太好了。
But it's kind of like if you think that could ever succeed, you should think that we're going to be extremely successful very soon because we have such a wonderful position to study this from.

383
00:32:17,540 --> 00:32:20,180
相比之下，这就像我们能克隆人一样。
Compared to that, it's as if we could clone people.

384
00:32:20,540 --> 00:32:20,820
是的。
Yes.

385
00:32:20,940 --> 00:32:29,020
而且还能克隆他们所处的确切环境，以及他们接收过的每一个输入，然后在实验中测试他们。
And also clone the exact environment that they're in and every input that's ever been given to them, and then test them in an experiment.

386
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:42,170
而神经科学显然如你所说有巨大的个体差异，还有人们一生中碰到的各种随机事件，以及实验中发生的事，也就是实验本身的噪声，对吧？
Whereas, you know, obviously neuroscience has massive, as you say, individual variation and also just random things that happen to people through their lives and things that happen in the experiment, the noise of the experiment itself, right?

387
00:32:42,170 --> 00:32:45,290
比如我们可以问模型同一个问题，一次给提示，一次不给提示。
Like, we could ask the model the same question, like, with and without a hint.

388
00:32:45,810 --> 00:32:53,770
但如果你把同一个问题问一个人三次，有时给提示，过一会儿他们就会开始意识到：上次你问我这个之后，你可是摇了摇头。
But if you ask a person the same question three times, like sometimes with a hint, after a while they start to understand, like, well, last time you asked me this, you really shook your head after that one.

389
00:32:54,130 --> 00:33:04,360
是的，我觉得，这种能把大量数据丢给模型、看哪些部分会亮起来，并且能跑大量实验去推动模型各个部分、观察会发生什么的能力……
Yes, I think, yeah, this kind of—this being able to just throw tons of data at the model and see what lights up, and being able to run a ton of these experiments where you're nudging parts of the model and seeing what happens.

390
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:20,520
我认为这让我们处在与神经科学相当不同的状态；神经科学中大量心血都花在想出非常巧妙的实验上，比如你只有……
I think is what puts us in a pretty different regime from neuroscience, and that a lot of, you know, blood and toil in neuroscience is spent coming up with really clever experiments, like you only have a—

391
00:33:20,520 --> 00:33:31,680
有限的时间和你的小鼠相处，否则它就会累；或者有人碰巧正在做脑外科手术，你赶紧趁头部打开时把电极放进去。
Certain amount of time with your mouse before it's, you know, gonna get tired, or, you know, someone happens to be having a brain surgery operation, so you quickly go in and put an electrode in their brain while their head's open.

392
00:33:31,880 --> 00:33:32,120
对。
Yeah.

393
00:33:32,320 --> 00:33:33,520
而这种情况不常发生。
And that doesn't happen very often.

394
00:33:33,720 --> 00:33:35,480
所以你必须得先猜一个。
And so you've got to come up with like a guess.

395
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:47,240
就像你在那里只有这么多时间，所以你必须得猜一下：我认为那个神经回路里正在发生什么？我能设计什么巧妙实验来检验那个精确的假设？
Like, you've only got so much time in there, and so you've got to come up with a guess of, like, what do I think is going on in that neural circuit, and what clever experimental design can I test that precise hypothesis?

396
00:33:47,810 --> 00:33:51,130
我们很幸运，基本不用那样做那么多。
And we're, we're very fortunate in that we kind of don't have to do that so much.

397
00:33:51,250 --> 00:33:55,570
我们可以差不多检验所有假设。
We can, we can just sort of test all the hypotheses.

398
00:33:55,890 --> 00:34:06,250
我们可以让数据告诉我们答案，而不是进去只检验某个非常具体的东西。我认为这在很大程度上让我们有能力发现那些让我们惊讶的……
We can kind of let the data speak to us rather than going in and testing some really specific thing, I think that's what's sort of unlocked a lot of our ability to find these things that are surprising to us that.

399
00:34:06,250 --> 00:34:07,690
那些我们事先猜不到的东西。
Like, we wouldn't have guessed in advance.

400
00:34:07,930 --> 00:34:10,530
如果你不得不那样做，你知道，这就很难做到。
That's hard to do if you, if you have to, you know.

401
00:34:11,470 --> 00:34:25,950
如果你的实验带宽有限，那什么是一个好例子，说明你进去打开或关闭这些概念中的一个，或者对模型做某种操控，从而揭示出模型思考方式的新东西？
If you have only a limited amount of experimental bandwidth, what's a good example then of you going in and switching one of these concepts on or off or doing some kind of manipulation of the model that then reveals something new about how the models are thinking.

402
00:34:26,190 --> 00:34:29,230
在最近的实验中，我们分享过一个让我很惊讶的例子。
In the recent experiments, we shared one that surprised me.

403
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:30,720
相当惊讶。
Quite a bit.

404
00:34:30,880 --> 00:34:37,840
嗯，它属于一条实验研究线；因为它很让人困惑，我感觉我们几乎就要说：好吧，我们不知道发生了什么。
Uh, and was part of sort of like an experimental line of work that, because it was confusing, I feel like we're on the verge of just saying, well, we don't know what's going on.

405
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:42,000
它大致就是那种提前规划几步的例子。
It's sort of this example of, um, like planning a few steps ahead.

406
00:34:42,160 --> 00:34:42,400
对。
Yes.

407
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:48,200
嗯，所以这个例子是，你让模型给你写一首诗，一首押韵的对句。
Uh, so this is the example of, you know, you give the model—you ask the model to write you a poem, a rhyming couplet.

408
00:34:48,400 --> 00:34:48,680
对。
Yeah.

409
00:34:48,840 --> 00:34:55,450
嗯，然后作为人类，如果你让我写押韵对句，比如说你甚至把第一行都给我了，我首先会想的是，啊……
Uh, and then, you know, as a human, if you ask me to write a rhyming couplet, and let's say you even give me the first line, the first thing I'll think of is sort of like, ah.

410
00:34:55,490 --> 00:34:56,610
好吧，我得押韵。
Well, you know, I need to rhyme.

411
00:34:57,490 --> 00:34:59,170
当前的押韵格式是这样的。
This is what the current rhyming scheme is.

412
00:34:59,250 --> 00:35:00,170
这些是可以选的词。
These are potential words.

413
00:35:00,290 --> 00:35:00,810
对。
Yeah.

414
00:35:00,810 --> 00:35:01,610
我是这么做的。
This is how I do it.

415
00:35:01,690 --> 00:35:08,370
再说，如果模型只是在预测下一个词，你不一定会预期它提前规划到第二……
And again, if the model was just predicting the next word, you wouldn't necessarily expect that it would be planning on to the sec-

416
00:35:08,570 --> 00:35:11,930
就是第二行末尾的那个词。
The, the, um, the word at the end of the second line.

417
00:35:12,130 --> 00:35:12,450
正是。
That's right.

418
00:35:12,450 --> 00:35:19,540
所以你会预期的默认行为、零假设是：模型看到你的第一行诗，然后它会说出第一个词。
And so the sort of default behavior you'd expect, the null hypothesis is like, well, the model sees your first verse and then it's gonna say the first word.

419
00:35:19,580 --> 00:35:21,780
在给定你所谈论内容的情况下，这么接一个词是合理的，然后继续。
That kind of makes sense given what you're talking about, keep going.

420
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:25,140
然后到最后，到了最后一个词时，它才会像：哦，我得跟这个押韵。
And then, you know, at the end, on the last word, it's gonna be like, oh, well, I need to rhyme with this thing.

421
00:35:25,180 --> 00:35:27,540
所以它会像是努力去凑一个押韵。
And so it's gonna sort of like try to fit in a rhyme.

422
00:35:27,740 --> 00:35:39,770
当然，这有时效果有限。如果在某些情况下，你只是说一句话而不考虑押韵，你会把自己逼入死角，到最后你就没法完成文本。记住，模型非常——
Of course, that only works so well, like in some cases, if you just say a sentence without thinking of the rhyme, you won't be able, you'll back yourself into a corner and at the end, you know, you won't be able to complete the text. And remember, the models are very.

423
00:35:39,810 --> 00:35:40,970
非常擅长预测下一个词。
Very good at predicting the next word.

424
00:35:41,010 --> 00:35:48,610
所以事实证明，要想在最后一个词上表现很好，你需要很早就想到那个词，就像人类一样。
So it turns out that to be very good at that last word, you need to have thought of that last word way ahead of time, just like humans do.

425
00:35:48,770 --> 00:35:58,690
所以当我们看这些诗的流程图时，模型其实在第一节结尾就已经选好了词。
And so it turns out that when we looked at these sort of flow charts for poems, the model had already picked the word at the end of the first verse.

426
00:35:59,170 --> 00:36:09,520
