You know, fake doing a math problem for you to, like, tell you what you want to hear.

527
00:44:40,770 --> 00:44:44,890
也许它们一直都在这么做，而我们只有看到它们脑袋里的活动才会知道。
Like, maybe they're just doing that all the time and we wouldn't know unless we kind of saw it in their heads.

528
00:44:45,490 --> 00:44:58,760
我认为这里有两个几乎独立的线索：一个是我们有很多方式，就像 Jack 说的，判断一个人可信的迹象是什么？但之前提到的 Plan A 这件事很关键。
I think there's two like almost separate strains here, like, and one is we have a lot of ways of like, yeah, I guess what Jack was saying, like, you know, what are the signs of trust in a human? But this like Plan A.

529
00:44:58,960 --> 00:45:09,480
之前提到的 Plan B 情况非常重要：可能你前 10 次或 100 次使用模型时，问的是某一类问题，而它一直处于 Plan A 的区域，然后……
Plan B thing from earlier is really important where like it might be that the first 10 or 100 times you use the model it was you're asking a certain kind of question, but it was like always in Plan A zone and then.

530
00:45:09,560 --> 00:45:14,560
你知道，你问它一个更难或不同的问题，它尝试回答的方式就完全不同。
You know, you ask it a harder or a different question and the way that it tries to answer it is just like completely different.

531
00:45:14,680 --> 00:45:28,990
它使用的是完全不同的策略集、不同的机制；这意味着它与你建立的信任，实际上是你与“在做 Plan A 的模型”建立的信任，而现在它像是在做 Plan B，可能会完全失控。
It's using a totally different set of strategies there like different mechanisms and that like that means that the trust it built with you was really your sort of trust with like model doing Plan A's and now it's like doing Plan B and like it's going to be completely off the rails.

532
00:45:29,430 --> 00:45:31,910
但你可能没有任何预警信号。
But like you didn't have like any warning sign of that.

533
00:45:32,070 --> 00:45:40,310
所以我觉得我们也想开始逐渐建立对模型如何做这些事情的理解，以便我们在这些领域形成某种信任基础。
And so it's sort of, I think we also just want to start building up an understanding of like, how do models do these things so that we can form like a trust basis in some of those areas.

534
00:45:40,350 --> 00:45:43,990
我认为，你可以在不完全理解一个系统的情况下与它建立信任。
And I think, like, you can form trust with a system you don't completely understand.

535
00:45:44,580 --> 00:45:53,940
但有点像，如果 Emmanuel 有个双胞胎，有一天 Emmanuel 的双胞胎来到办公室，我没有……我心想：这看起来是同一个人，结果他在电脑上做了完全不同的事。
But you sort of like if it's just like Emmanuel had a twin and then like one day like Emmanuel's twin came to the office and like I didn't like— I was like, this seems like the same guy and then just did something completely different on the computer.

536
00:45:54,420 --> 00:45:54,660
对。
Right.

537
00:45:54,700 --> 00:45:57,580
那可能会出问题，取决于那是不是邪恶的双胞胎。
Like that could go south depending on if it was the evil twin.

538
00:45:57,980 --> 00:45:59,260
是的，嗯。
Yes, well.

539
00:45:59,300 --> 00:45:59,940
或者是善良的双胞胎。
Or the good twin.

540
00:45:59,940 --> 00:46:00,620
嗯，是啊，显然。
Well, yeah, obviously.

541
00:46:01,580 --> 00:46:05,580
哦，我以为你会问我是不是那个邪恶双胞胎。我不打算回答这个。是的。
Oh, I thought you were gonna ask me if I was the evil twin. I'm not gonna answer that. Yes.

542
00:46:07,270 --> 00:46:13,790
在讨论开始时，我问过，语言模型是否像人类一样思考？
At the start of this discussion, I asked, you know, is a language model thinking like a human?

543
00:46:15,270 --> 00:46:18,270
我很想听听你们三位各自的回答。
I'd be interested to hear an answer from all three of you.

544
00:46:18,390 --> 00:46:20,070
你们认为这在多大程度上成立。
The extent to which you think that's true.

545
00:46:21,790 --> 00:46:26,310
这问题真让我为难。但我认为它……它是在思考。
Put me on the spot with that one. But I think it's... it's thinking.

546
00:46:27,190 --> 00:46:28,190
但不是像人类那样思考。
But not like a human.

547
00:46:29,310 --> 00:46:31,150
不过这不是一个很有用的回答。
But that's not a very useful answer.

548
00:46:31,190 --> 00:46:34,510
也许可以再深入一点。
So maybe to dig in a little bit more.

549
00:46:34,990 --> 00:46:40,510
嗯，说它在思考似乎是个挺深刻的说法，对吧，因为再说一次，它只是在预测下一个词。
Well, it seems like quite a profound thing to say that it's thinking, right, because again, it's just predicting the next word.

550
00:46:40,670 --> 00:46:45,590
有些人认为它们只是自动补全，但你的意思是它实际上在思考，我想是的。
Some people think that these are just autocompletes, but you're saying that it is actually thinking, I think, yeah.

551
00:46:45,590 --> 00:46:49,270
也许补充一点我们还没谈到、但我认为很重要的内容。
So maybe to add something that we haven't touched on yet, but I think is really important.

552
00:46:50,430 --> 00:47:01,430
要理解与语言模型对话的实际体验，关键在于：我们一直在说预测下一个词，但在你与语言模型对话的语境里，这到底意味着什么？
For understanding actual experience of talking to language models, is that like we're talking about predicting the next word, but what does that actually mean in the context of a dialogue that you're having with a language model?

553
00:47:02,030 --> 00:47:02,750
是什么？
It's what?

554
00:47:02,870 --> 00:47:11,870
真正在底层发生的是，语言模型在填充一份你与它创造的这个角色之间的对话记录。
What's really going on under the hood is that the language model is filling in a transcript between you and this like character that it's created.

555
00:47:12,150 --> 00:47:21,010
所以在语言模型的设定世界里，你被称为 human，格式就像 human 冒号，后面接你写的内容。
So in, in the, like, canon world of the language model, you are called human and you're—it's like human colon, the thing you wrote.

556
00:47:21,410 --> 00:47:23,490
然后还有一个叫 assistant 的角色。
And then there's this character called the assistant.

557
00:47:23,930 --> 00:47:31,450
我们训练模型，让 assistant 这个角色具备某些特质，比如乐于助人、聪明、友善。
And we've like trained the model to imbue the assistant with certain characteristics, like being helpful and, like, smart and nice.

558
00:47:32,170 --> 00:47:36,290
然后它在模拟这个 assistant 角色会对你说什么。
And then it's like simulating what this assistant character would say to you.

559
00:47:36,860 --> 00:47:40,700
所以在某种意义上，我们确实像是按照自己的形象创造了这些模型。
So in, in a sense, we, we really have, like, created the models in our image.

560
00:47:40,860 --> 00:47:46,140
我们实际上是在训练它们去扮演这种类似人形机器人的角色。
We are literally training them to like cosplay as this sort of humanoid robot character.

561
00:47:46,820 --> 00:47:56,260
所以在这个意义上，为了预测这个友善、聪明的人形机器人角色会如何回答你的问题。
And so in that sense, like, well, in order to predict what this like nice smart humanoid robot character would say in response to your question.

562
00:47:57,100 --> 00:48:00,320
如果你非常擅长这个预测任务，你需要做到什么？
What do you have to do if you're really good at that prediction task?

563
00:48:00,440 --> 00:48:07,800
你必须在一定程度上形成一种内部模型，表示这个角色在表达什么，或者说它在“想”什么。
You have to kind of form this internal model of like what that character is representing, like what it's, what it's thinking, so to speak.

564
00:48:07,800 --> 00:48:15,840
所以，为了完成预测 assistant 会说什么的任务，语言模型某种程度上需要形成对 assistant 思维过程的模型。
So, like, in order to do its task of predicting what the assistant would say, the language model kind of needs to form this model of the assistant's thought process.

565
00:48:16,360 --> 00:48:28,520
我觉得从这个意义上说，“语言模型在思考”其实只是一个非常功能性的说法：为了做好扮演这个角色这件事，它们需要这样。
And I think, like, in that sense, it—like, the just—the claim that, like, language models are thinking is really just—it's this very, like, functional claim of just, in order to do their job of kind of, like, playing this character well.

566
00:48:28,720 --> 00:48:38,440
它们需要去模拟这个过程——不管我们人类在思考时做的到底是什么——而且它的模拟很可能与我们大脑的工作方式非常不同。
They need to sort of simulate the process—whatever it is that we humans are doing when we're thinking—and its simulation is very likely quite different from how our brains work.

567
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:39,440
但它在某种程度上是在尝试。
But it's kind of trying.

568
00:48:39,560 --> 00:48:41,200
就像朝着同一个目标努力。
It's like shooting towards the same goal.

569
00:48:42,400 --> 00:48:47,080
我觉得这个问题有点情感成分——当你问“它们是否像我们一样思考”时。
I think there's kind of an emotional part to this question or something when you ask, are they thinking like us?

570
00:48:47,200 --> 00:48:49,940
就像在问，我们是不是没那么特别，或者类似的意思？
It's like, are we not that special or, or something?

571
00:48:49,980 --> 00:49:01,220
而且我觉得，在我和那些读过论文或不同解读文章的人讨论我们聊到的一些数学例子时，这一点变得很明显——就是那个例子，你知道。
And, and I think—I think it's been apparent to me discussing some of the, some of the math examples that we're talking about with, with people that have engaged with, like, read the paper or, or, or different write-ups, which is this example where, you know.

572
00:49:01,220 --> 00:49:04,420
我们让模型回答 36 + 59 等于多少。
We asked a model to say 36 + 59, what's the answer?

573
00:49:04,980 --> 00:49:07,700
模型能正确回答。
And the model can correctly answer it.

574
00:49:07,820 --> 00:49:10,380
你也可以问它：那你是怎么做到的？
You can also ask it, well, how do you, how do you do that?

575
00:49:10,460 --> 00:49:23,160
它会说：哦，你知道，我把 6 和 9 相加，然后进了一位，再把所有的十位数相加。但事实证明，如果我们去看它内部，这根本不是它在做的事。它没那样做。所以它又……
And it'll say, oh, you know, I added the 6 and the 9 and then I carried the one and then uh, I added all the, the sort of like tens digits, but it turns out that if we look inside the brain, like, we can, that's not at all what it's doing. It didn't do that. So again, it.

576
00:49:23,160 --> 00:49:23,880
是在胡扯。
Was bullshitting you.

577
00:49:23,920 --> 00:49:24,360
它又一次。
It was again.

578
00:49:24,600 --> 00:49:25,000
做了些事情。
Did things.

579
00:49:25,080 --> 00:49:25,400
没错。
That's right.

580
00:49:25,400 --> 00:49:26,240
它又在胡扯了。
Again, it was bullshitting you.

581
00:49:26,240 --> 00:49:35,440
它实际做的其实是一种挺有意思的混合策略：并行处理十位数和个位数，并进行一系列不同的步骤。
What it actually does is actually this sort of kind of interesting mix of strategies where it's in parallel doing the tens digit and the ones digit and sort of doing so like a series of different steps.

582
00:49:35,840 --> 00:49:42,400
但有意思的是，在和人聊起这件事时，大家对这意味着什么反应不一。
But the thing that's interesting here is that talking to people, so like, I think the reaction is split on, on like what does that mean?

583
00:49:43,000 --> 00:49:47,400
某种意义上，我觉得很酷的一点是，这类研究有一部分是不带观点的。
And in a sense, I think what's cool is some of this research is like free of opinion.

584
00:49:47,480 --> 00:49:48,760
我们只是告诉你，这就是发生的事情。
We're just telling you like, this is what happened.

585
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:54,880
你可以随意据此得出结论，认为模型在思考或不在思考。
You can—you feel free to, you know, from that, from that conclude that the model is thinking or is not thinking.

586
00:49:54,960 --> 00:49:59,980
一半的人会说，它告诉你它进位了，但它并没有这么做。
And half of the people will say like, well, you know, it told you that it was carrying the one and it didn't.

587
00:49:59,980 --> 00:50:01,900
所以显然，它甚至不理解自己的想法。
And so clearly, it doesn't even understand its own thought.

588
00:50:01,940 --> 00:50:03,140
所以显然它没有在思考。
And so clearly it's not thinking.

589
00:50:03,700 --> 00:50:10,540
而另一半人会说，嗯，当你问我 36 + 59 时，我也有点……我知道结果末尾是 5。
And then half of the other people will be like, well, you know, when you ask me 36 + 59, I also kind of, you know, I know that it ends at five.

590
00:50:10,580 --> 00:50:12,820
我知道结果大概在八九十左右。
I know that it's roughly like in the 80s or 90s.

591
00:50:13,140 --> 00:50:15,940
呃，我脑子里有所有这些启发式方法，就像我们之前聊到的。
Uh, I have all of these heuristics in my brain as we were talking about.

592
00:50:16,020 --> 00:50:17,500
我不确定自己具体是怎么算出来的。
I'm not sure exactly how I compute it.

593
00:50:17,500 --> 00:50:19,780
我可以把它写出来，用一步步的方式计算。
I can write it out and compute it, you know, the longhand way.

594
00:50:20,240 --> 00:50:25,800
但我大脑里实际发生的方式有点模糊和奇怪，可能和那个例子中模型内部发生的情况有类似的模糊和奇怪。
But the way that it's happening in my brain is sort of like fuzzy and weird, and it might be similarly fuzzy and weird to what's happening in that example.

595
00:50:26,040 --> 00:50:36,440
人类在元认知方面是出了名地差，也就是思考自己的思考、理解自己的思维过程，尤其是在那些即时、反射性的回答中。
Humans are notoriously bad at metacognition, like thinking about thinking and understanding their own thinking processes, especially in cases where it's, you know, immediate reflexive answers.

596
00:50:36,600 --> 00:50:40,800
那我们凭什么要求模型不一样呢？
So why should we expect any different for models?

597
00:50:41,110 --> 00:50:43,150
嗯，Josh，你对这个问题的回答是什么？
Um, Josh, what's your answer to the question?

598
00:50:43,990 --> 00:50:44,950
我赞同 Emmanuel 的说法。
I like Emmanuel.

599
00:50:45,030 --> 00:50:47,470
我要回避这个问题，然后大概会问：为什么？
I'm gonna avoid the question and just sort of be like, what?

600
00:50:47,590 --> 00:50:49,150
为什么这么问？
Why do you ask?

601
00:50:49,150 --> 00:50:49,470
我不知道。
I don't know.

602
00:50:49,470 --> 00:50:51,790
这有点像在问：手榴弹会像人一样出拳吗？
It's sort of like asking like, does a grenade punch like a human?

603
00:50:52,110 --> 00:50:53,990
就像，不。
Like, like, no.

604
00:50:53,990 --> 00:50:55,670
嗯，确实有某种力，是的。
Well, there's some force, yes.

605
00:50:55,790 --> 00:51:07,760
呃，所以，也许还有比这更接近的东西，但如果你担心的是破坏力，那我觉得，理解冲击来自哪里、它的推动力是什么，也许才是……
Uh, so, you know, and maybe there are things that are closer than that, but like if you're worried about damage, then I think, I think understanding, you know, where does the impact come from, what is the impetus of this is, is maybe like the.

606
00:51:08,160 --> 00:51:19,160
我觉得对我来说，重要的一点是：模型会思考吗？从它们确实会做整合、处理和顺序推进的事情，并能走向出人意料的结果这个意义上说，显然是。
The important thing I think for me, the like, do models think in the sense that they like do some like integration and processing and sequential stuff that can lead to surprising places, clearly.

607
00:51:19,320 --> 00:51:19,640
是的。
Yes.

608
00:51:20,290 --> 00:51:25,330
经常和它们互动之后，要是还说里面没有发生什么，那才有点奇怪。
Um, it'd be kind of crazy from interacting with them a lot for there not to be something going on.

609
00:51:25,410 --> 00:51:26,890
我们某种程度上开始看到它是如何发生的。
We can sort of start to see how it's happening.

610
00:51:27,010 --> 00:51:32,130
然后“像不像人”这部分很有趣，因为我觉得其中一些是在问：我能对它们抱什么预期？
Then the like humans bit is interesting because I think some of that is trying to ask like, you know, what can I expect from these?

611
00:51:32,330 --> 00:51:35,250
因为这有点像：我擅长这件事，就会让它也擅长那件事。
Because it was sort of like me being good at this would make it good at that.

612
00:51:35,250 --> 00:51:38,930
但如果它和我不同，那我就真的不太知道该找什么。
But if it's like different from me, then like, I don't really know what to sort of look for.

613
00:51:38,930 --> 00:51:54,160
所以实际上我们只是想弄清楚：在哪些地方我们需要极其警惕，或者在理解时需要从零开始；在哪些地方我们可以在某种程度上直接借鉴我们自己非常丰富的思考经验。而在这一点上，我觉得有点被困住了。
And so really we're just looking to, like, understand, like, where do we need to be extremely, like, suspicious or like starting from scratch in understanding this and where can we sort of just reason from, like, our own very rich experience of thinking? And there I feel a little bit trapped.

614
00:51:54,240 --> 00:51:58,680
因为作为人类，我不停地把自己的形象投射到一切事物上。
Because as a human, I project my own image constantly onto everything.

615
00:51:58,840 --> 00:52:02,280
就像《圣经》里警告过我们的那样，我就像这块硅片。
Like they warned us in the Bible, where I'm just like this piece of silicon.

616
00:52:02,680 --> 00:52:08,890
它就像按我的形象造出来的我；在某种程度上，它被训练来模拟人与人之间的对话。
It's just like me made in my image where, where like to some extent it's been trained to like simulate dialogue between people.

617
00:52:08,930 --> 00:52:11,290
所以它在表现上会非常像人。
So it's gonna be very like person like in its affect.

618
00:52:12,290 --> 00:52:18,730
所以仅从训练过程来看，就会有一些人性进入其中；但随后它使用的是非常不同的底层机制，有着不同的限制。
And so some humanness will get into it simply from like the training, but then it's like using very different equipment that has, like, different limitations.

619
00:52:18,770 --> 00:52:21,930
所以它做这件事的方式可能会非常不同。
And so the way it does that might be pretty different to.

620
00:52:21,970 --> 00:52:23,290
回应 Emmanuel 的观点，我觉得……
To Emmanuel's point, I think the.

621
00:52:24,010 --> 00:52:27,250
是的，我们回答这类问题时处在很棘手的境地，因为……
Yeah, we're in this tricky spot answering questions like this because.

622
00:52:28,080 --> 00:52:32,000
我们还没有合适的语言来谈论语言模型所做的事。
We don't really have the right language for talking about what language models do.

623
00:52:32,080 --> 00:52:46,320
这就像我们是在做生物学，但是是在人们发现细胞或 DNA 之前的那种生物学。我觉得我们正在开始填补这种理解，就像 Emmanuel 说的，现在有些情况下我们真的可以、我们可以……
It's like we're we're doing biology, but, you know, before people figured out cells or before people figured out DNA, I think we're starting to fill in that understanding like, like, you know, as Emmanuel said, there are these cases now where we can really just, we can just.

624
00:52:46,360 --> 00:52:51,880
如果你直接去读我们的论文，你就会知道模型是如何把这两个数字相加的，然后你想管这叫人类也可以。
If you just go read our paper, like you'll know how the model like added these two numbers and then you want to call it human.

625
00:52:51,880 --> 00:52:55,120
比如你想叫它思考，或者不想叫，那都随你。
Like if you want to call it thinking or if you want to not, then it's up to you.

626
00:52:55,320 --> 00:53:00,880
但真正的答案其实就是找到合适的语言和合适的抽象来谈论这些模型。
But like the real answer is just like find the right language and the right abstractions for talking about the models.

627
00:53:01,120 --> 00:53:12,760
但与此同时，当我们目前只完成了这个科学项目大概 20% 的时候，为了填补剩下 80%，我们不得不从其他领域借用类比。
But in the meantime, when we when we've only currently we've only kind of like, you know, 20% succeeded at that scientific project, like to fill in the other 80%, we sort of have to borrow analogies from other fields.

628
00:53:12,880 --> 00:53:15,820
于是就有个问题：哪些类比最贴切。
And, like, there's this question of which analogies are the most apt.

629
00:53:15,980 --> 00:53:18,340
我们应该把模型想成计算机程序吗？
Should we be thinking of the models like computer programs?

630
00:53:18,540 --> 00:53:20,900
我们应该把它们想成小人吗？
Should we be thinking of them like little people?

631
00:53:21,620 --> 00:53:26,500
看起来有时候，在某些方面，把它们想成小人还挺有用的。
And it seems to be like sometimes, like in some ways, thinking of them like little people is kind of useful.

632
00:53:26,740 --> 00:53:29,620
比如，如果我对模型说一些刻薄的话，它会回嘴。
It's, like, if I, like, say mean things to the model, it, like, talks back at me.

633
00:53:29,860 --> 00:53:31,100
那就像人会做的那样。
That's like a human would do.

634
00:53:31,220 --> 00:53:32,540
但在某些方面，它确实像那样。
But in some ways, it's like that.

635
00:53:32,540 --> 00:53:33,940
显然不是正确的心智模型。
Clearly not the right mental model.

636
00:53:33,980 --> 00:53:38,480
所以我们就有点卡住了，需要弄清楚什么时候该借用哪种语言。
And so we're just kind of stuck, you know, figuring out when, when we should be borrowing which language.

637
00:53:38,760 --> 00:53:41,920
那么，这就引出了我本来想问的最后一个问题：接下来是什么？
Well, that that leads on to the final question I was gonna ask, which is what's next?

638
00:53:42,240 --> 00:53:53,040
接下来需要取得哪些科学进展、生物学进展，才能让我们更好地理解这些模型内部在发生什么，并且，呃，再次……
What are you what, what are the next, uh, pieces of scientific progress, biological progress that need to be made for us to have a better understanding of what's happening inside these models and, uh, again.

639
00:53:53,120 --> 00:53:55,780
朝着我们让它们更安全的使命。
Towards our mission of making them safer.

640
00:53:56,620 --> 00:53:57,780
还有很多工作要做。
There's a lot of work to do.

641
00:53:59,100 --> 00:54:05,000
我们上一篇论文有很大一部分在讨论我们目前研究方法的局限。
Our, our, our, our last publication has some like enormous section on, on the limitations of the way we've been looking at this.

642
00:54:05,040 --> 00:54:07,120
那也是一个让方法变得更好的路线图。
That was also a roadmap to like making it better.

643
00:54:07,200 --> 00:54:16,760
当我们寻找模式来分解模型内部发生了什么时，我们所能获得的可能只占实际发生情况的百分之几。
You know, we when we when we are looking for patterns to like decompose what's happening inside the model, we're only getting sort of, you know, maybe a few percent of what's of what's going on.

644
00:54:17,480 --> 00:54:23,920
它在如何移动信息方面，有很大一部分我们完全没有捕捉到。
There's large parts of how it moves information around that like we, we explicitly like didn't capture at all.

645
00:54:24,540 --> 00:54:33,060
嗯，我们正在从我们所用的那种较小的生产模型向上扩展——比如你们看的是 Claude。
Um, they're scaling this up from, from the sort of small, you know, uh, production model we use to like, so you were looking at Claude.

646
00:54:33,300 --> 00:54:34,340
Claude 3.5 Haiku。
Claude 3.5 Haiku.

647
00:54:34,540 --> 00:54:34,900
没错。
That's right.

648
00:54:34,900 --> 00:54:37,100
它其实是一个相当有能力的模型。
Which is like, it's like a pretty capable model.

649
00:54:37,180 --> 00:54:41,260
非常快，但远没有 Claude 4 那么复杂精密。
Very fast, but it's like by no means as sophisticated as, as you know, the Claude 4.

650
00:54:41,380 --> 00:54:42,900
对，Claude 4 整个系列模型。
Yeah, suite, suite of models.

651
00:54:43,380 --> 00:54:51,340
嗯，所以这些几乎算是技术层面的挑战，但我想 Emmanuel 和 Jack 可能对解决这些之后会遇到的科学挑战有一些看法。
Um, so those are almost like sort of like technical challenges, but I think, I think Emmanuel and Jack might have some takes on the like, some of the, like, scientific challenges that come after solving those.

652
00:54:51,380 --> 00:54:51,620
是的。
Yeah.

653
00:54:52,030 --> 00:54:52,270
是的。
Yeah.

654
00:54:52,270 --> 00:55:02,510
我想也许我会在这里说两件事。Josh 所说的一个后果是，在我们提出问题的所有次数中……
I mean, I think maybe, maybe two things I'll say here, which is one consequence of what Josh said is that, you know, uh, out of the total number of times that we ask a question.

655
00:55:03,030 --> 00:55:04,710
关于模型如何做某件事。
Uh, about how the model does X.

656
00:55:04,950 --> 00:55:09,470
目前我们能回答的可能只有大概 10% 到 20% 的情况。
Right now we can answer probably a small, you know, 10 to 20% of the time.

657
00:55:09,510 --> 00:55:12,070
经过一点调查后，我们可以告诉你：这就是正在发生的事。
We can tell you after a little bit of investigation, this is what's happening.

658
00:55:12,500 --> 00:55:14,220
显然，我们希望这个比例能高得多。
Obviously, we'd like that to be a lot better.

659
00:55:14,220 --> 00:55:18,620
而且有很多比较清晰的方法可以达到那个目标。
And there's, there's a lot of kind of clearer ways to, to, to get there.

660
00:55:18,660 --> 00:55:20,620
也有更审慎和更投机的方法。
And, and less and more speculative ways as well.

661
00:55:21,020 --> 00:55:30,890
呃，然后我觉得我们常常谈到的一件事是：模型做的很多事并不只是像“它接下来要怎么说”这么简单。
Uh, and then I think a thing that we've talked a lot about is this sort of idea that a lot of what the model does isn't simply like, ah, how is it saying the next thing?

662
00:55:30,970 --> 00:55:32,210
我们这里已经稍微谈过这一点。
We talked about it a little bit here.

663
00:55:32,290 --> 00:55:35,930
它有点像提前规划几件事，或者提前想好几个词。
It's sort of like planning a few things again and a few words ahead.

664
00:55:36,010 --> 00:55:36,290
抱歉。
Sorry.

665
00:55:36,650 --> 00:55:45,730
而且我认为我们想理解，在与模型进行长时间对话时，它对正在发生的事情的理解是如何变化的。
And I think we want to understand sort of like over a long conversation with the model, sort of like, how is its understanding of what's happening changing?

666
00:55:45,810 --> 00:55:50,410
比如，它对“自己在和谁对话”的理解如何变化，以及这又如何影响它的行为。
You know, how is its understanding of who it's talking to changing and how does that affect its behavior?

667
00:55:51,370 --> 00:56:01,010
越来越多地，像 Claude 这样的模型的实际使用场景是，它会读一堆你的文档、一堆你发的邮件或你的代码。
More and more sort of the actual use case of models like Claude is, you know, it's going to read a bunch of your documents and a bunch of like email you sent or your code.

668
00:56:01,050 --> 00:56:02,690
然后基于这些内容给出一个建议。
And based on that, it's going to make one suggestion.

669
00:56:02,730 --> 00:56:06,770
所以很明显，在它阅读所有这些内容的这个过程中，有一些非常重要的事情在发生。
And so clearly, there's something really important happening in that space where it's reading all these things.

670
00:56:07,450 --> 00:56:11,170
所以我认为，更好地理解这一点似乎是一个值得迎接的重大挑战。
And so I think understanding that better seems like a great challenge to take on.

671
00:56:11,620 --> 00:56:11,900
Jack。
Jack.

672
00:56:12,260 --> 00:56:18,900
是的，我想我们团队经常用这样一个类比：我们正在打造一台用来观察模型的显微镜。
Yeah, I think we often use the analogy on the team that we're building a microscope to, like, look at the model.

673
00:56:19,060 --> 00:56:36,650
而现在，我们处于一个既令人兴奋又有点令人沮丧的阶段：我们的显微镜大约只有 20% 的时候能正常工作，而且要通过它观察需要很多技巧，还需要，你知道，搭建一整套复杂的装置之类。
And right now, we're in this exciting but also kind of frustrating space where our microscope works like 20 percent of the time and, like, looking through it, like, requires a lot of skill and, like, takes, you know, you have to, like, build this whole big contraption and everything.

674
00:56:36,770 --> 00:56:38,050
基础设施总是出问题。
Like, infrastructure is always breaking.

675
00:56:38,490 --> 00:56:46,770
然后，一旦你得到了关于模型在做什么的解释，你还得把 Emmanuel 或我，或者团队里的某个人，关进一个房间里花上大约两个小时来……
And then, like, once you've got your, like, explanation of what the model's doing, you have to, like, throw, like, Emmanuel or me or someone else on the team in a room for, like, two hours to, like.

676
00:56:46,810 --> 00:56:52,890
琢磨清楚到底发生了什么。而我认为，我们可能在……你知道，一两年内到达的那个非常令人兴奋的未来。
Puzzle out what exactly was going on and like the really exciting future that I think we could be at within, you know.

677
00:56:53,910 --> 00:57:01,720
一两年，你知道，在那种时间尺度上，你与模型的每一次交互都可以被放到显微镜下。
A year or two years, you know, that kind of time scale is, is one where, like, just every interaction you have with the model can be under the microscope.

678
00:57:01,840 --> 00:57:13,560
就像我们可以，每当模型做了这些奇怪的事情，我们只希望按一下按钮，对，你正在和它对话，你按个按钮就能得到一张流程图，告诉你它刚才在想什么。
Like we can just, anytime there's all these weird things the models are doing and we just want it to be like push of a button like, yeah, you're having your conversation, you push a button you get this flowchart that tells you like what it was thinking about.

679
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:27,140
一旦到了那个阶段，我想我们在 Anthropic 的可解释性团队会开始呈现出一些不同的形态，不再是一支主要由工程师组成的团队。
And once we're at that point, it'll be this like I think our, the interpretability team at Anthropic, I think will start to kind of take on a bit of a different shape and that instead of this like team of kind of like engineers.

680
00:57:27,660 --> 00:57:32,300
科学家们思考语言模型内部如何工作的那种数学机制。
Scientists thinking about the, like, math of how, like, language models work on the inside.

681
00:57:32,780 --> 00:57:44,060
我们会拥有一支像生物学家一样的大军，他们只是透过显微镜观察；我们与 Claude 对话，让它做出各种奇怪的事情，然后让一些人透过显微镜观察它内部在想什么。
We're gonna have this, like, army of biologists that are just looking through the microscope, that we're just, we're talking to Claude, we're getting it to do weird things and then we're just, like, we got people looking through the microscope seeing, like, what it was thinking on the inside.

682
00:57:44,060 --> 00:57:46,550
我认为这大概就是这项工作的未来。
I think that's kind of the future of, of, of this work.

683
00:57:46,790 --> 00:57:47,070
好。
Nice.

684
00:57:47,230 --> 00:57:48,790
也许对此补充两点。
Maybe two, two notes on that.

685
00:57:48,790 --> 00:57:53,390
一是，我们希望 Claude 帮我们完成所有这些，因为这涉及很多环节。
One is like, we want Claude to help us do all of that because, like, there's a lot of parts involved.

686
00:57:53,510 --> 00:57:57,830
而且你知道，擅长同时查看数百个东西、弄清楚发生了什么的是 Claude。
And, you know, who's, like, good at, like, looking at, like, hundreds of things and figuring out what's going on is, like, Claude.

687
00:57:57,990 --> 00:58:00,590
所以我们正尝试在这方面争取一些帮助。
And so I think we're trying to enlist some help there.

688
00:58:00,590 --> 00:58:02,950
嗯，尤其是在这些复杂语境下。
Um, especially for these complicated contexts.

689
00:58:03,310 --> 00:58:08,950
也许另一点是，我们已经谈了很多关于研究模型在完全成型后的情况。
And maybe the, the other place is like, we've talked a lot about studying the model like once it's fully formed.

690
00:58:09,030 --> 00:58:11,070
但当然，我们所在的公司就是制造这些模型的。
But of course, like we're at a company that makes these.

691
00:58:11,600 --> 00:58:19,480
所以当我们说，好，这是模型如何解决这个特定问题或说出这句话的，它在训练过程中是从哪里来的？
And so when it says, okay, here's how the model, like, solved this particular problem or said this thing, where did that come from kind of in the training process?

692
00:58:19,720 --> 00:58:29,880
是哪些步骤促使那种计算回路形成并做到这些？以及我们如何能给公司里其他正在做这些工作的人提供反馈，去塑造我们真正……
What are the steps that sort of like made that circuitry sort of form to do that and how could we give feedback to the rest of the company that is like doing all of that work to shape the thing that we like actually?

693
00:58:30,840 --> 00:58:31,560
希望它成为的样子。
Want it to become.

694
00:58:32,000 --> 00:58:33,840
好的，非常感谢这次对话。
Well, thank you so much for the conversation.

695
00:58:34,200 --> 00:58:36,440
大家可以在哪里了解更多关于这项研究的信息？
Where can people find out more about this research?

696
00:58:37,040 --> 00:58:43,720
如果想了解更多，你可以访问 Anthropic.com/research，上面有我们的论文、博客文章和相关有趣视频。
So if you want to find out more, you can go to Anthropic.com/research, which has our papers and blog posts and fun videos about it.

697
00:58:44,160 --> 00:58:49,760
另外，我们最近与一个叫 Neuronpedia 的团队合作，托管了我们制作的一些回路图。
Also, we recently partnered with another group called Neuronpedia to host some of these like circuit graphs we make.

698
00:58:49,800 --> 00:58:57,760
如果你想亲自尝试看看一个小模型内部发生了什么，可以去 Neuronpedia 自己体验。非常感谢。
So if you want to try your hand at looking at what's going on inside of a small model, you can go to Neuronpedia and see for yourself. Thank you very much.
